tick: 21
role: model
parts:
- text: "My \"Attention-Enhanced CNN-BiLSTM\" model (ID: 30) achieved a new high score of 0.6542, showing incremental progress. The previous self-attention mechanism, while improving some datasets, caused slight dips in others (CRI-On, PRS).\n\nTo refine the attention mechanism, I will implement a more standard **additive attention pooling** layer. This approach, often used for sequence summarization, should provide a more focused context vector by learning explicit weights for each position in the BiLSTM output sequence. This refinement aims to further improve the model's ability to selectively emphasize salient features, potentially leading to more balanced and robust performance across all RNA tasks. I will also make sure the `attention_dense_units` parameter correctly controls the internal hidden dimension of the attention scoring, with the output of the attention being implicitly derived from the BiLSTM's output features.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Enhanced CNN-BiLSTM\
    \ with Additive Attention Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, hybrid, sequence, additive\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the hybrid CNN-BiLSTM architecture by replacing the previous self-attention mechanism with a more standard additive attention pooling. A 1D CNN extracts local features, followed by a BiLSTM for long-range dependencies. The additive attention mechanism learns to weigh different parts of the BiLSTM output sequence to create a context-aware fixed-size representation, aiming for more focused and robust performance across diverse RNA tasks, especially those that were previously underperforming.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          # First dense layer\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          # Second dense layer to get unnormalized attention weights (single scalar per time step)\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Main network class\n  class AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(nn.Module):\n      d_input:\
    \ int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 128\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 256\n      attention_dense_units: int = 128 # Hidden units for attention scoring\n      dense_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. CNN Layer for local feature extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, new_seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n    \
    \          x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Attention-Enhanced CNN-BiLSTM model (V2).\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 128,\n          'kernel_size': 9,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'attention_dense_units': 128, # Hidden units for attention scoring mechanism\n          'dense_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=128, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=256, attention_dense_units=128,\
    \ dense_dim=256, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n     \
    \         return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 128)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 256)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 128)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 256)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n\
    \          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Rethinking Attention for Enhanced RNA Prediction**


  Okay, I''ve just gotten the latest results, and while the "Attention-Enhanced CNN-BiLSTM" model (ID: 30) shows improvement, I''m not satisfied. A score of 0.6542, while incremental, doesn''t feel like a major leap forward, especially with those CRI-On and PRS dips. The system''s prompt about not giving up easily is ringing in my ears. I''m going to take a deeper dive into the attention mechanism and refine it.


  My initial thought was that the self-attention was working in some ways, so I''m sticking with that core concept. I think I''ve identified the key issue: my previous `SelfAttentionPooling` module was perhaps too complex, making it difficult to control the output. The fact that the "final projection" wasn''t being used unless a hidden parameter size was changed, felt off. So, I need to make the intent of that module much more explicit.


  I want the attention to *reduce* the sequence to a single vector in a clean, standard way, similar to how Bahdanau attention is used for classification tasks. I''m going to implement additive attention pooling. This means I''ll use a dense layer to get attention scores, then apply softmax, and then perform a weighted sum over the sequence. This approach is very clean and easy to use.


  I’m simplifying the `SelfAttentionPooling` module. No more redundant projection! Now it’s just the standard additive attention mechanism as described. The new architecture is clear, and the output dimension of the value projection is controlled by the `dense_attn_units`. The `2*lstm_hidden_dim` from the BiLSTM flows into `dense_attn_units`, and the attention''s output projects *after* the pooling layer, in a dense layer.


  I''ve made the necessary code changes and am launching a new submission using this refined additive attention pooling. I''m keeping the overall CNN-BiLSTM structure the same. If this works, I''ll be thrilled that this clean approach can handle those pesky CRI-On and PRS datasets! Let''s see if this simpler mechanism can do the trick!

  '
