tick: 25
role: model
parts:
- text: "My \"Tuned Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\" (ID: 42) achieved a new high score of 0.6724. While performance on most datasets is strong, CRI-Off continues to be a challenge, and I noticed that an earlier pure CNN model achieved a better CRI-Off score. This suggests that the choice of pooling mechanism might be critical for specific tasks.\n\nTo address this and potentially improve overall robustness, I will now implement a hybrid pooling strategy. This approach will combine the strengths of both additive attention pooling (for context-aware summarization) and global max pooling (for extracting the most salient features). By concatenating the outputs of these two pooling methods, the model will have a richer, more diverse representation of the sequence before making its final predictions. I will also adjust the dense layer capacities to accommodate the larger pooled representation.\n\nThis experiment represents a deeper exploration into how sequence information\
    \ is summarized and aims to improve performance across all datasets by providing a more comprehensive sequence representation.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Pooling CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention & Max Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, maxpooling, hybrid, sequence\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the hybrid CNN-BiLSTM architecture by introducing a hybrid pooling strategy. After the CNN and BiLSTM layers, both additive attention pooling and global max pooling are applied to the BiLSTM output. The resulting representations are concatenated and fed into a two-layer dense network with increased capacity. This aims to leverage both context-aware summarization and salient feature extraction for improved and balanced performance across all diverse RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention\
    \ pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Main network class\n  class\
    \ HybridPoolingCNNBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim_1: int = 512 # Increased for concatenated features\n      dense_dim_2: int = 256 # Added second dense layer\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. CNN Layer for local feature extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output\
    \ = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x_bilstm = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, new_seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Hybrid Pooling: Additive Attention and Global Max Pooling\n          attention_pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x_bilstm) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x_bilstm, axis=1) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n        \
    \  x = jnp.concatenate([attention_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (batch, 4 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim_1)(x) # (batch, dense_dim_1)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim_2)(x) # (batch, dense_dim_2)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Hybrid Pooling CNN-BiLSTM model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features':\
    \ 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim_1': 512,\n          'dense_dim_2': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_HybridPooling:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Hybrid Pooling.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192,\n                   dense_dim_1=512, dense_dim_2=256, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = HybridPoolingCNNBiLSTMNetwork(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim_1=dense_dim_1, dense_dim_2=dense_dim_2, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n\
    \          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"\
    kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim_1 = hparams.get(\"dense_dim_1\", 512)\n      dense_dim_2 = hparams.get(\"dense_dim_2\", 256)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_HybridPooling(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim_1=dense_dim_1, dense_dim_2=dense_dim_2, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Rethinking the Pooling Strategy**


  Okay, so I just got the results back for submission 42, the Tuned Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling, and the overall score hit a new personal best, 0.6724! That''s a solid improvement. But looking at the individual scores, while most are up, CRI-Off is still stubbornly low. It''s time to dig deeper.


  I''ve been incrementally improving this hybrid architecture, but I''m starting to see diminishing returns. The system''s tip about questioning fundamentals has me thinking. Maybe the problem isn''t just deeper layers or more complex attention. Maybe the *way* I''m summarizing the sequence information is the bottleneck. The current pooling mechanisms, whether it''s global mean or additive attention, seem to be losing crucial details, especially for those short sequences in CRI-Off.


  RNA sequences are complex, with intricate structures and functionally important motifs. My current models capture local and long-range dependencies, but then they squeeze that information into a single vector. This aggregation might be too lossy. I need to consider alternative pooling approaches or methods for combining information.


  The problem statement asks for unified architectures, and I''ve been achieving this via the final dense layers, but the core feature extraction is the same for all tasks. This begs the question: is it really the best approach for every subtask?


  Positional information is another aspect I haven''t directly addressed. While CNNs are somewhat translation-invariant, and LSTMs process sequentially, explicit positional encodings, like in Transformers, could be beneficial. But a full Transformer is a big undertaking.


  Instead of fixed pooling, what if I allow the model multiple output representations? Or, if pooling is the issue, are there alternative methods for summarizing sequence data?


  Let''s focus on CRI-Off, the CRISPR off-target prediction task. The max sequence length is only 43. Short sequences might struggle to develop meaningful receptive fields when using deep CNNs, and LSTMs will also likely struggle to capture the full context with such short sequences. Aggressive global pooling might be too lossy if specific positions are important.


  My architectures may be too generic, too "unified". Maybe an adaptive or more nuanced pooling mechanism is needed. Multi-head attention is a possibility, but complex. I think I''ll try some simpler experiments first.


  Currently, the additive attention computes a weighted sum. The output is a single context vector. What if I try something else? Max pooling, for instance, which might be more robust to noise and highlight the most active features along the sequence, seems like a good first step. I could even concatenate mean and max pooling to retain both types of information.


  Spearman correlation (CRI-Off''s metric) is rank-based. My loss functions are MSE/BCE. Perhaps I need to explore different loss functions or make sure my model''s output aligns better with ranks.


  Given the diminishing returns and the system''s tip, I''m going to step back and rethink my approach. My current setup is CNN -> BiLSTM -> AdditiveAttentionPooling -> Dense -> Output, with specific parameter configurations. The additive attention currently produces a single context vector, but what if I try a different summary method?


  A quick look at past models reveals that a 1D CNN with global max pooling had a better CRI-Off score than my current model. That suggests a CNN may be superior, or that global *max* pooling is beneficial. My additive attention pooling is essentially a weighted average. Max pooling extracts the most prominent feature, which could be important for motif detection, especially on short sequences.


  My next step: a hybrid pooling approach! I''ll keep the CNN-BiLSTM feature extractor but modify the pooling.


  Here''s the plan:

  1. Keep the CNN and BiLSTM layers.

  2. After the BiLSTM output, perform *both* additive attention and global max pooling.

  3. Concatenate the outputs of these two pooling mechanisms.

  4. Feed the concatenated vector into the subsequent dense layers.


  This will increase the dimensionality, so I''ll need to adjust my dense layers.


  I''m aiming for an ablation study of sorts, directly targeting the pooling mechanism, and hopefully, this will unlock a breakthrough. Let''s see if this multi-pronged approach to sequence summarization can move the needle!

  '
