tick: 27
role: model
parts:
- text: "My last submission with hybrid pooling (ID: 46) resulted in a slightly lower overall score (0.6582) compared to the previous best (ID: 42, 0.6724), although it did significantly improve `ncRNA_Accuracy`. This indicates that simply adding more pooling mechanisms might not lead to a universal improvement and can sometimes dilute the overall signal for other tasks.\n\nTherefore, I will revert to the architecture of my best-performing model so far, the \"Tuned Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\" (ID: 42). I will then make a targeted adjustment: I will increase the capacity of the final dense layer to `512` units (from `384`). This aims to provide the model with more expressive power in its final stages of processing the context-aware representation from the attention mechanism, without fundamentally altering the successful CNN and BiLSTM sequence processing.\n\nThis approach follows the \"don't give up on an approach too easily\" tip, focusing on refining a proven architecture.\n\
    \n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Refined Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Increased Dense Capacity)\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, hybrid, tuning, capacity\"\nabstract: \"Building upon the successful Tuned Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (ID: 42), this submission increases the hidden dimension of the final dense layer from 384 to 512. This targeted adjustment aims to enhance the model's capacity to learn intricate patterns from the attention-pooled sequence representation, striving for further improvements in overall performance across all RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def\
    \ __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Main network class\n  class AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n    \
    \  lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512 # MODIFIED: Increased capacity from 384 to 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. CNN Layer for local feature extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim),\
    \ name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, new_seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\
    \ # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Attention-Enhanced CNN-BiLSTM model (Tuned).\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512, # MODIFIED\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Tuned).\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n         \
    \     conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Focusing on Refinement: The Path to Improvement**


  Okay, so the "Hybrid Pooling CNN-BiLSTM" model (ID 46) didn''t deliver the breakthrough I''d hoped for.  While it *did* show a significant leap in the `ncRNA_Accuracy` metric, the overall score dipped below my previous best, the "Tuned Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling" (ID 42).  That 0.6724 score from ID 42 is the target. The hybrid pooling approach seems to have hurt the other metrics.


  My initial thought was that the hybrid pooling might have been a bit too disruptive, diluting the nuanced information that the additive attention pooling method captured so effectively in ID 42. It''s a trade-off, and it seems this trade was not beneficial. The tip about not abandoning an approach too early resonates here. The architecture of ID 42 was, after all, my most successful thus far.


  Therefore, my plan is to revert to the architecture of ID 42 – a "Tuned Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling" – and then carefully refine it.  The idea is to give the existing architecture a bit more ''oomph'' without overhauling the successful pooling strategy.


  Specifically, I''m going to slightly increase the capacity of the dense layer after the additive attention pooling. My initial impulse was to try a deeper network.  But looking at the changes I made to ID 46, it seems that was not the right move. I will stick to the same BiLSTM architecture and additive attention pooling from ID 42, but I''ll increase the dimensionality of the single dense layer following it.  In ID 42, this `dense_dim` was 384; I''ll bump it up to 512, keeping the *dropout* rate at 0.2, as in ID 42.  This gives the model more parameters to learn from the pooled representation.


  Here are the key values for the revised network:

  - `conv_features`: 192 (unchanged)

  - `kernel_size`: 9 (unchanged)

  - `lstm_hidden_dim`: 384 (unchanged)

  - `attention_dense_units`: 192 (unchanged)

  - `dense_dim`: 512 (increased from 384)

  - `dropout_rate`: 0.2 (unchanged)


  The hope is that this targeted increase in capacity will allow the model to better leverage the features learned through the attention pooling, potentially pushing the score beyond 0.6724. I''ll be interested to see how these changes influence the individual metric scores as well. Specifically, I''ll be keeping an eye on the `APA_R2`, and `MRL_R2` scores, to see if they can be improved.

  '
