tick: 29
role: model
parts:
- text: "My current best model (ID: 51) achieved a score of 0.6738, an incremental improvement by increasing dense layer capacity. While generally strong, the performance on some datasets, notably CRI-Off, suggests that a more nuanced approach to sequence summarization might be beneficial. Simply increasing layer capacity or hybridizing pooling methods did not yield universal improvements.\n\nFollowing the system's advice to \"question the fundamentals\" and brainstorm \"novel ideas,\" I will implement a **Multi-Head Additive Attention Pooling** mechanism. This approach will allow the model to learn multiple, independent context vectors from the BiLSTM output, rather than a single aggregated representation. Each \"head\" will apply an additive attention mechanism, enabling the model to focus on different aspects of the sequence simultaneously. Concatenating these diverse context vectors should provide a richer, more comprehensive summary of the RNA sequence, potentially leading to more robust\
    \ performance across all diverse tasks.\n\nThis is a significant architectural change to the pooling layer, moving towards a more sophisticated method of information aggregation.\n\nMy next submission will include:\n1.  **Update `_define_hyperparameters()`**: Introduce `num_attention_heads` (e.g., 4) and `attention_head_units` (e.g., 128) for the internal dimension of each attention head.\n2.  **Implement `SingleHeadAdditiveAttention(nn.Module)`**: A sub-module for a single additive attention mechanism.\n3.  **Implement `MultiHeadAdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module)`**: This module will instantiate and run multiple `SingleHeadAdditiveAttention` modules in parallel on the BiLSTM output, then concatenate their results.\n4.  **Update `AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V3(nn.Module)`**: Integrate the `MultiHeadAdditiveAttentionPooling` after the BiLSTM layers, followed by two dense layers (to handle the increased dimensionality from concatenated heads) before the task-specific output.\n\n`/execute_action{submit\
    \ 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Head Additive Attention CNN-BiLSTM for RNA Modeling\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, multihead, hybrid\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a Multi-Head Additive Attention Pooling mechanism into the CNN-BiLSTM hybrid architecture. After the 1D CNN for local feature extraction and the BiLSTM for sequential dependencies, multiple independent additive attention heads are applied to the BiLSTM output. The concatenated context vectors from these heads provide a richer, multi-faceted sequence summary, aiming to capture diverse salient features and achieve more robust and higher performance across all seven RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Single Additive Attention Head module\n  class SingleHeadAdditiveAttention(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\
    \      head_name: str # Unique name for this head\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=f\"{self.head_name}_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=f\"{self.head_name}_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Multi-Head Additive Attention Pooling module\n  class MultiHeadAdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      num_attention_heads:\
    \ int\n      attention_head_units: int # Hidden units for each attention head's scoring\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          head_outputs = []\n          for i in range(self.num_attention_heads):\n              head_output = SingleHeadAdditiveAttention(\n                  dense_attn_units=self.attention_head_units,\n                  head_name=f\"attention_head_{i}\"\n              )(x)\n              head_outputs.append(head_output)\n          \n          # Concatenate outputs from all heads\n          # (batch, num_attention_heads * input_features)\n          return jnp.concatenate(head_outputs, axis=-1)\n\n  # Main network class\n  class AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V3(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      num_attention_heads: int\
    \ = 4 # NEW\n      attention_head_units: int = 128 # NEW (internal dense units for each head)\n      dense_dim_1: int = 512 # First dense layer after pooling\n      dense_dim_2: int = 256 # Second dense layer\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. CNN Layer for local feature extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n\
    \          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, new_seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Multi-Head Additive Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = MultiHeadAdditiveAttentionPooling(\n              num_attention_heads=self.num_attention_heads,\n              attention_head_units=self.attention_head_units\n          )(x) # (batch, num_attention_heads * 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim_1)(x) # (batch, dense_dim_1)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim_2)(x) # (batch, dense_dim_2)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Multi-Head Additive Attention CNN-BiLSTM model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'num_attention_heads': 4, # NEW\n          'attention_head_units': 128, # NEW\n          'dense_dim_1': 1024, # Adjusted for increased input from concatenated attention heads (4 * 2 * 384 = 3072 features if lstm_hidden_dim\
    \ is 384, so 1024 is reasonable)\n          'dense_dim_2': 512, # Adjusted\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_MultiHeadAttention:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Multi-Head Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, num_attention_heads=4, attention_head_units=128,\n                   dense_dim_1=1024, dense_dim_2=512, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V3(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, num_attention_heads=num_attention_heads,\n              attention_head_units=attention_head_units,\n              dense_dim_1=dense_dim_1, dense_dim_2=dense_dim_2, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n  \
    \        )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"\
    kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      num_attention_heads = hparams.get(\"num_attention_heads\", 4)\n      attention_head_units = hparams.get(\"attention_head_units\", 128)\n      dense_dim_1 = hparams.get(\"dense_dim_1\", 1024)\n      dense_dim_2 = hparams.get(\"dense_dim_2\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_MultiHeadAttention(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, num_attention_heads=num_attention_heads,\n          attention_head_units=attention_head_units,\n          dense_dim_1=dense_dim_1, dense_dim_2=dense_dim_2, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Architectural Shift: Towards Multi-Head Attention for Enhanced Sequence Summarization**


  Okay, so I''ve hit a performance plateau. Increasing the capacity of the final dense layer helped a little, but it feels like I''m just tweaking around the edges at this point. Time to take a step back and *really* think about what''s going on.


  My current models are good, hitting decent scores across the board, but they all use CNNs, BiLSTMs, or hybrids, with some form of pooling at the end. CRI-Off is consistently a problem, which tells me the problem is fundamental. The system tips are pretty clear: "question the fundamentals."


  The common thread in all these models is how they go from a variable-length sequence to a fixed-size vector.  Current methods are just *summarizing* the sequence, but is that enough? What if the summary is too restrictive? RNA''s 3D structure means that sequence order isn''t everything. Information might be encoded across the *entire* sequence, not just locally.


  So, I’m rethinking the pooling step.  Instead of just getting one summary vector, how about having the model "see" the sequence in multiple ways? The previous self-attention wasn''t multi-head. This is where multi-head attention might be the answer.  It can provide multiple "glimpses" of the sequence, each focusing on a different aspect, leading to a richer representation.


  My plan now is to implement a **Multi-Head Additive Attention Pooling** mechanism.  It''s a novel component and represents a substantial architectural shift, not just hyperparameter tuning. Instead of a single attention mechanism, I''ll have several "heads", each with its own set of attention weights, producing individual context vectors. These vectors will be concatenated, giving the model a much more complex summary to work with.


  Essentially, I''ll create `num_heads` additive attention modules, each independently attending to the input sequence and producing its own context vector. These will then be concatenated. I will make a `MultiHeadAdditiveAttentionPooling` module, and a sub-module `SingleHeadAdditiveAttention` that will simply perform the previous additive attention logic.


  This requires defining new hyperparameters (`num_attention_heads`, `attention_head_units`) and adjusting the final dense layers to handle the concatenated outputs. This change should make the model learn multiple context vectors from the sequence, potentially improving the model''s ability to capture the diverse patterns present in the data.


  I''m feeling good about this. This is the kind of critical thinking the system was suggesting, a real architectural improvement, not just more tweaking. This should allow for a more nuanced and powerful way to summarize the sequence information for the model.

  '
