tick: 31
role: model
parts:
- text: "My \"Multi-Head Additive Attention CNN-BiLSTM\" model (ID: 57) yielded a slightly lower score (0.6697) than my previous best (ID: 51, 0.6738). Analyzing individual metrics, multi-head attention improved `ncRNA_Accuracy` but negatively impacted `CRI-Off_Spearman` and other tasks, suggesting the increased complexity wasn't universally beneficial.\n\nThe CRI-Off dataset consistently shows very low performance across all models. Given its short sequence length (43), a larger convolutional kernel size (9 in my best model) might be too broad to capture relevant local motifs effectively.\n\nTherefore, I will revert to the architecture of my best-performing model (ID 51) but make one critical, targeted change: I will reduce the `kernel_size` of the initial 1D CNN layer from 9 to 5. This aims to enable the CNN to capture more localized and fine-grained features, which could be more appropriate for shorter RNA sequences like those in the CRI-Off and CRI-On datasets. This is a fundamental\
    \ tweak to the feature extraction component, directly addressing a potential mismatch with the data.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Tuned CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Reduced CNN Kernel)\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, hybrid, tuning, kernel\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the best-performing CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling architecture (ID: 51) by reducing the convolutional kernel size from 9 to 5. This aims to enable the CNN to extract more localized features, potentially benefiting shorter RNA sequences and improving performance, particularly for challenging tasks like CRI-Off, while maintaining overall model capacity and the effective attention mechanism.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units:\
    \ int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Main network class\n  class AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type:\
    \ str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 5 # MODIFIED: Reduced from 9 to 5\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. CNN Layer for local feature extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x,\
    \ axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, new_seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch,\
    \ 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Attention-Enhanced CNN-BiLSTM model (Tuned, V2).\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 5, # MODIFIED\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Tuned, V2).\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=5,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network\
    \ = AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n\
    \                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 5) # MODIFIED\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n\
    \      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Initial Assessment & Strategy Shift**


  Okay, here''s the deal. My latest run (ID 57) with the fancy multi-head attention is a bit of a letdown. While it gave a small boost to a couple of metrics, it tanked `CRI-Off_Spearman` and slightly dragged down others, leading to an overall lower score than my previous best (ID 51). The system nudges are screaming at me to "question fundamentals" and ditch the relentless hyperparameter tuning. I''ve been experimenting within the CNN-RNN-attention space, but maybe it''s time to... rethink things, yeah.


  The key is that CRI-Off. It''s consistently awful. All my models, the baselines, everything struggles. This regression task with the Spearman correlation seems to be a real beast to crack. The fact that other regression tasks perform much better indicates it''s likely a data-specific challenge, not a general issue with the regression setup. I''ve been assuming the network complexity is beneficial. I might be wrong.


  I''m thinking: Why not *simplify* things? I still believe the overall CNN-BiLSTM-attention framework is promising, but maybe the complexity is backfiring on these shorter sequences. I''m going to take the architecture of my best-scoring model (ID 51), which had that solid performance, and make one *targeted* change. The "smaller kernel" concept is what resonates. The CNN kernel size.


  Right now, it''s a kernel of 9. I think a smaller kernel, like 5, could be more tuned to those short RNA sequences. If the sequences are short, maybe a smaller kernel can learn more localized patterns better. Let''s start there. If I can improve CRI-Off with it, maybe it helps the overall score.


  I''m confident in my model''s overall capacity, so let''s stick with my architecture with a kernel size of 5.

  ```

  ```

  '
