tick: 33
role: model
parts:
- text: "My previous submission (ID: 64) showed a slight overall decrease in score but a notable improvement in `CRI-Off_Spearman` after reducing the CNN kernel size. This indicates that different datasets, particularly those with varying sequence lengths, might benefit from distinct architectural configurations. Relying on a single set of hyperparameters across all seven diverse RNA datasets is proving to be a limiting factor.\n\nTo address this, I will implement an **adaptive hyperparameter strategy** within the `create_network` function. This approach will allow the model to dynamically adjust the CNN's `kernel_size` based on the specific `dataset` currently being processed by the Ray Tune trial. This aligns with the principle of \"creating novel components from first principles based on domain knowledge,\" as RNA sequence lengths and characteristics vary significantly between tasks.\n\nSpecifically, I will:\n1.  **Revert to the overall architectural parameters of my best model (ID 51)**,\
    \ which was \"Refined Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Increased Dense Capacity)\".\n2.  **Modify `create_network`**:\n    *   For short-sequence datasets (`CRI-Off`, `CRI-On`), I will set a smaller `kernel_size` (e.g., 5) to capture fine-grained local patterns.\n    *   For the very long-sequence dataset (`APA`), I will use a larger `kernel_size` (e.g., 11) to capture broader motifs.\n    *   For all other medium-length sequences, I will use a default `kernel_size` (e.g., 7), as a compromise.\n3.  The `_define_hyperparameters` will provide a default `kernel_size` (e.g., 7) that can be overridden by the adaptive logic in `create_network`.\n\nThis dynamic `kernel_size` adjustment aims to optimize local feature extraction for each dataset, leading to more robust and balanced performance across all RNA prediction tasks.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adaptive CNN Kernel Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention,\
    \ hybrid, adaptive, kernel\"\nabstract: \"This submission implements an adaptive hyperparameter strategy within the best-performing CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention architecture. The convolutional kernel size is dynamically adjusted based on the input dataset's characteristics (e.g., shorter kernel for CRI-Off/CRI-On, longer for APA). This aims to optimize local feature extraction for each diverse RNA sequence type, leading to more robust and higher overall performance across all seven prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention\
    \ scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Main network class (unchanged from ID 51, takes kernel_size dynamically)\n  class AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 7 # Default value, will be overridden\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n  \
    \    attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. CNN Layer for local feature extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n\
    \          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, new_seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\
    \n  # Hyperparameter definition - provides base values, kernel_size is dynamic\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define base hyperparameters for the Adaptive CNN Kernel model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 7, # Default kernel size, to be overridden by adaptive logic\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=7, # Default to 7\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output,\
    \ task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size, # Pass dynamic kernel size\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                  \
    \    rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      dataset_name = hparams.get('dataset')\n      # Dynamically set kernel size based on dataset sequence length characteristics\n      if dataset_name in [\"CRI-Off\", \"CRI-On\"]:\n          actual_kernel_size = 5 # Smaller kernel for short sequences\n      elif dataset_name == \"APA\":\n          actual_kernel_size = 11 # Larger kernel for very long sequences\n      else:\n          actual_kernel_size = 7 # Default/medium for others\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot\
    \ RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=actual_kernel_size, # Pass the dynamic kernel size\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Adapting the CNN Kernel Size for Diverse RNA Datasets**


  Okay, so looking at the results from "Tuned CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Reduced CNN Kernel)" (ID: 64), I see it''s a bit of a mixed bag. The overall score is a touch lower than my best model (ID 51), but the CRI-Off Spearman correlation did see a nice jump, which supports my hunch that a smaller kernel works better for short sequences or to capture those localized patterns. The trade-off is that other metrics decreased slightly, which tells me I''m not going to get a universal "best" model with a single set of hyperparameters. This gets me thinking about the underlying nature of these diverse RNA datasets. They''re all different lengths, have different tasks, and probably have different biological patterns at play. A single, static approach just isn''t going to cut it.


  The plan now is to build on this insight and go for a more adaptable strategy. Since the submission system is already running parallel trials for each dataset, I can leverage that to optimize the model for *each* dataset individually. The key here is to make the hyperparameter selection *context-aware*. That aligns perfectly with the Codex guidelines – specifically, the need to be strategic and use domain knowledge. My domain knowledge here is the variable nature of RNA datasets.


  Here''s the refined plan: I will go back to the base architecture of ID 51, which performed well overall. The big change will be in how I define the hyperparameters. The original idea was to modify `_define_hyperparameters()` to return a *conditional* set of hyperparameters, but I realize the system doesn''t work that way. `_define_hyperparameters()` is static. It turns out that `_define_hyperparameters` can''t directly use dataset names, and the dataset information is only present in `create_network` which receives the `hparams` dictionary.


  So, the new approach will be as follows:


  1.  **`_define_hyperparameters()`**: I will define the global set of hyperparameters here including `learning_rate`, `conv_features`, `lstm_hidden_dim`, `attention_dense_units`, `dense_dim`, and `dropout_rate`. I will set `kernel_size` as a default value, for now I will set it to 7, which is a compromise between the very small and large values.

  2.  **`create_network(hparams)`**: Inside the `create_network` function, I will check the `hparams[''dataset'']` value, which is available! Based on the dataset name, I''ll then dynamically set the `kernel_size` parameter before instantiating the network. Specifically, I''ll adjust the `kernel_size` based on the sequence length of each dataset: smaller kernels for shorter sequences and larger ones for longer sequences.


  This way, the network *adapts* itself on the fly to the specific dataset it''s being trained on. I''ll test smaller kernels for CRI-Off and CRI-On and larger kernels for APA. I''ll make sure to pass the dynamic kernel size to the `CustomRNANetwork_AttentionCNNBiLSTM_V2` constructor. So, by creating a new `create_network` function to control the dynamic hyperparameter choice, I am creating an *adaptive* network.


  This will be a significant change from my earlier submissions, shifting from a "one-size-fits-all" hyperparameter approach to one that leverages the specific characteristics of each dataset. This is a much better way to extract maximum performance from each individual task. Let''s call this the "Adaptive CNN Kernel with Additive Attention".

  '
