tick: 35
role: model
parts:
- text: "My previous attempts have shown that while a hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with additive attention is effective, achieving universal top performance across all seven diverse RNA datasets remains challenging. The adaptive kernel size improved CRI-Off but at the cost of other tasks, indicating that a single global hyperparameter choice for the CNN is suboptimal.\n\nThe system's guidelines allow for \"final layers to be tailored for each prediction type.\" Currently, only the very last dense layer's output dimension adapts. To implement more substantial tailoring, I will introduce a **task-specific multi-head prediction architecture** after the shared CNN-BiLSTM-Attention feature extractor. This means instead of one generic dense layer before the output, there will be distinct dense pathways for each major `task_type` (regression, multi-label classification, multi-label regression, multi-class classification). This allows each prediction head to specialize in the patterns relevant to its specific\
    \ task, potentially leading to more balanced and robust performance.\n\nI will revert to the core hyperparameters of my best-performing model (ID 51) for the shared feature extractor (CNN kernel size 9, `conv_features`, `lstm_hidden_dim`, `attention_dense_units`). Then, I'll add separate, dedicated dense layers for each `task_type` to enhance specialization.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Task-Specific Head CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, multihead, task-specific\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention architecture by implementing task-specific prediction heads. After the shared feature extraction (CNN, BiLSTM, Additive Attention Pooling), the model branches into separate dense layer pathways for each unique task type (regression, multi-label classification, multi-label regression, multi-class classification). This allows specialized learning for each prediction problem,\
    \ aiming to achieve more balanced and improved performance across all seven diverse RNA datasets.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1)\
    \ # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Main network class with task-specific heads\n  class TaskSpecificHeadCNNBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      \n      # Task-specific dense layer dimensions\n      regression_dense_dim: int = 512\n      multilabel_clf_dense_dim: int = 512\n      multilabel_reg_dense_dim: int = 512\n      multiclass_clf_dense_dim: int = 512\n\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. CNN Layer for local feature extraction\
    \ (shared)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling (shared)\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch,\
    \ new_seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation (shared)\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Task-Specific Dense Layers and Output Heads\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(self.regression_dense_dim, name=\"regression_dense_1\")(x)\n              x = nn.relu(x)\n              x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n              x = nn.Dense(1, name=\"regression_output\")(x)\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_classification\":\n              x = nn.Dense(self.multilabel_clf_dense_dim, name=\"multilabel_clf_dense_1\")(x)\n              x = nn.relu(x)\n              x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name=\"\
    multilabel_clf_output\")(x)\n              return x\n          elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(self.multilabel_reg_dense_dim, name=\"multilabel_reg_dense_1\")(x)\n              x = nn.relu(x)\n              x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name=\"multilabel_reg_output\")(x)\n              return x\n          elif self.task_type == \"multiclass_classification\":\n              x = nn.Dense(self.multiclass_clf_dense_dim, name=\"multiclass_clf_dense_1\")(x)\n              x = nn.relu(x)\n              x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name=\"multiclass_clf_output\")(x)\n              return x\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown task_type: {self.task_type}\")\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters\
    \ for the Task-Specific Head CNN-BiLSTM model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'regression_dense_dim': 512,\n          'multilabel_clf_dense_dim': 512,\n          'multilabel_reg_dense_dim': 512,\n          'multiclass_clf_dense_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_TaskSpecificHeads:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Task-Specific Head CNN-BiLSTM.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192,\n                   regression_dense_dim=512, multilabel_clf_dense_dim=512,\n                   multilabel_reg_dense_dim=512, multiclass_clf_dense_dim=512,\n                   dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network =\
    \ TaskSpecificHeadCNNBiLSTMNetwork(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              regression_dense_dim=regression_dense_dim,\n              multilabel_clf_dense_dim=multilabel_clf_dense_dim,\n              multilabel_reg_dense_dim=multilabel_reg_dense_dim,\n              multiclass_clf_dense_dim=multiclass_clf_dense_dim,\n              dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n          \
    \    return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      regression_dense_dim = hparams.get(\"regression_dense_dim\", 512)\n      multilabel_clf_dense_dim = hparams.get(\"multilabel_clf_dense_dim\", 512)\n      multilabel_reg_dense_dim = hparams.get(\"multilabel_reg_dense_dim\", 512)\n      multiclass_clf_dense_dim\
    \ = hparams.get(\"multiclass_clf_dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_TaskSpecificHeads(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          regression_dense_dim=regression_dense_dim,\n          multilabel_clf_dense_dim=multilabel_clf_dense_dim,\n          multilabel_reg_dense_dim=multilabel_reg_dense_dim,\n          multiclass_clf_dense_dim=multiclass_clf_dense_dim,\n          dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis and Next Steps for Model Optimization**


  Okay, so I just got the results back for my "Adaptive CNN Kernel Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention" model (ID: 67). The overall score is 0.6644, which is a bit lower than my previous best (ID: 51 at 0.6738). Let''s dive into this.


  The individual metrics are a mixed bag. APA_R2, Modif_AUC-ROC, CRI-On_Spearman, PRS_R2, MRL_R2, and ncRNA_Accuracy are all slightly down compared to ID 51 and/or ID 42. However, CRI-Off_Spearman is my highest score *ever* on this metric, which is a significant win. The adaptive kernel clearly helped there, but at the cost of the other datasets. This confirms my initial hypothesis about kernel size, but a single network isn''t optimizing for all of them.


  The system tips are pushing me to think more critically and strategically, to question my core approach. Currently, I''m trying to force a single architecture to handle a diverse set of datasets. The constant trade-offs suggest that the *shared* core feature extraction might be the bottleneck.


  The rules encourage a similar architecture, but allow for tailoring the final layers. The adaptive kernel was one step in that direction, but I can go further. Given the challenge, perhaps a *more* modular, multi-headed approach is necessary for the final prediction layers.


  Right now, `create_network` returns a single instance that''s used for all datasets. I pass `d_output` and `task_type` as hyperparameters, so I *can* adjust the final dense layer. But the CNN, BiLSTM, and Attention layers are shared. I need to make the *final* layers more task-specific.


  The problem specifically asks to explore architectures that can do both regression and classification. My current approach does handle both, but only *implicitly*. I''m focusing on "the final dense layers can be tailored". Currently, `nn.Dense(self.d_output)` handles the output dimension; but what if the classification and regression tasks had *separate* branches?


  Looking at my best performing model (ID 51), I see the performance is more balanced. CRI-Off, which the new model improves significantly, is only slightly below my best. I need to keep the CRI-Off gain while recovering the performance on the other tasks.


  I''m going to take this system tip literally. Instead of a single final `nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)` followed by `nn.Dense(self.d_output)`, I''m going to branch out after the attention pooling with *separate* dense heads for the major `task_type` categories: regression, multi-label classification, multi-label regression, and multi-class classification. This will allow for the intermediate dense layers to specialize for each output task.


  My plan is to create a "multi-head" prediction head *after* the shared feature extractor. The pooling output will feed into separate dense branches, each tailored to a specific `task_type`.


  I''ll be modifying `AttentionCNNBiLSTMNetwork_V2` (from submission ID 51) to have multiple output heads. The pooling output (from AdditiveAttentionPooling) will feed into a series of distinct dense layers: separate pathways and output dimensions for each task type. For example: a regression branch with Dense_Regression_1 -> ReLU -> Dropout -> Dense_Regression_Output, etc. Each branch can have its own `dense_dim` and `d_output`, with full architectural flexibility.


  I will keep the base feature extraction parameters from ID 51 (conv_features: 192, kernel_size: 9, lstm_hidden_dim: 384, attention_dense_units: 192, dense_dim: 512, dropout_rate: 0.2). I will fix the kernel size to the default (9), since the overall best performing model used that setting. Then, I''ll modify the final layers to have the multi-headed structure.


  I''ll try setting the following hyperparameters:

  - `learning_rate`: 0.001

  - `conv_features`: 192

  - `kernel_size`: 9

  - `lstm_hidden_dim`: 384

  - `attention_dense_units`: 192

  - `regression_dense_dim`: 512

  - `multilabel_clf_dense_dim`: 512

  - `multilabel_reg_dense_dim`: 512

  - `multiclass_clf_dense_dim`: 51'
