tick: 37
role: model
parts:
- text: "My \"Task-Specific Head CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention\" (ID: 72) yielded a score of 0.6675, which is slightly lower than my best-performing model (ID: 51, 0.6738). While task-specific heads improved some metrics, they also caused a decline in others, indicating that this level of specialization might not be universally beneficial across the diverse datasets.\n\nFollowing the system's advice to \"question the fundamentals\" and \"not endlessly grinding on recombination,\" I will revert to the core architecture and hyperparameters of my best model (ID 51). However, I will introduce a more fundamental architectural change to the *initial feature extraction* stage: a **Residual Convolutional Block**. This block will consist of two 1D convolutional layers with `SAME` padding, connected by a residual skip connection. This allows for deeper and more stable learning of local sequence motifs by facilitating gradient flow, which should enhance the features passed to the BiLSTM and additive\
    \ attention pooling.\n\nThis change aims to improve the quality of the initial sequence representation, which is a critical foundation for all subsequent processing, without over-complicating the later stages of the model.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, residual, hybrid, architecture\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a Residual Convolutional Block as the initial feature extractor in a CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling architecture. Comprising two 1D convolutional layers with a skip connection and 'SAME' padding, this block aims to learn richer, more stable local sequence motifs. These enhanced features are then processed by the BiLSTM and additive attention, striving for improved and robust performance across all diverse RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n\
    \  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n    \
    \      return pooled_output\n\n  # Residual Convolutional Block\n  class ResidualConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      conv_features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic):\n          # x_input shape: (batch, seq_len, input_features)\n          input_features = x.shape[-1]\n          residual = x\n          \n          # 1st Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_1\")(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2nd Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_2\")(y)\n          \n          # Project input features to match conv_features if necessary for residual connection\n          if input_features != self.conv_features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(features=self.conv_features,\
    \ name=\"residual_projection\")(residual)\n          \n          # Add residual connection and apply final activation/dropout\n          x = nn.relu(residual + y)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n          return x\n\n  # Main network class\n  class ResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. Residual CNN Block for local feature extraction\n          x = ResidualConvBlock(\n              conv_features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n      \
    \    )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # Output shape: (batch, seq_len, conv_features)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n  \
    \        x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9, \n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512,\n\
    \          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_ResidualCNN:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = ResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                  \
    \     dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"\
    dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_ResidualCNN(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Recent Results and Charting a New Course**


  I''ve just reviewed the latest evaluation results, and while the "task-specific head" approach didn''t quite deliver the breakthrough I was hoping for, it has given me valuable insights. The overall score dipped slightly, and while performance improved on some tasks, the gains were offset by decreases in others. This highlights a need to re-evaluate fundamental assumptions about the model''s architecture.


  My gut feeling is that the issue isn''t with the task-specific heads themselves, but rather with the increased complexity and potential for suboptimal training or initialization. The system message about "questioning the fundamentals" resonates strongly. I''ve spent significant time refining architecture, hyperparameters, and attention mechanisms, but a major leap forward remains elusive. It''s time to shift gears and re-examine the core challenge: modeling RNA sequences.


  The current approach, even with my best model (ID 51), starts with a simple convolutional layer, which might be too basic for initial feature extraction. Instead of focusing on incremental adjustments, I''m now contemplating a more fundamental change – building a richer initial representation of the RNA sequence. This could involve using a stack of CNN layers before the BiLSTM to capture more complex local motifs, or even exploring the secondary structure of RNA, which is not directly captured in a linear input.


  My most successful model so far has a hybrid structure: CNN for local features, BiLSTM for context, additive attention for summarization, and a dense layer for prediction. All the hyper-parameters for this structure have been optimized and the only difference between ID 42 and ID 51 was increasing the `dense_dim` to 512.


  To push past the current limitations, I''m planning to introduce a **residual connection** within the convolutional block. This should facilitate deeper learning by allowing easier gradient flow, potentially leading to more sophisticated local feature extraction. I will revert to the parameters of ID 51.


  Here''s the plan: I will replace the single convolutional layer with a residual block, which looks like this: `Conv -> ReLU -> Dropout -> Conv -> Add(original_input) -> ReLU -> Dropout`. This will create a more robust CNN block and is in line with the suggestion to change something more fundamental. I''ll preserve the core structure and hyperparameters from my best-performing model (ID 51). Specifically, this will be defined by a `ConvBlock` using padding ''SAME''. I''ll also add a dense layer to project the input dimensions to match the output from the convolutional layers in the residual block. I''ll carefully manage shapes so the residual connections are valid.


  This new architecture will be the basis for my next submission. I am optimistic that this fundamental change will enable a significant performance improvement.

  '
