tick: 39
role: model
parts:
- text: "My \"Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\" (ID: 77) achieved a new high score of 0.6771. The residual convolutional block successfully enhanced initial feature extraction, particularly for `ncRNA_Accuracy` and `CRI-Off_Spearman`. However, `PRS_R2` slightly decreased, indicating that a single, fixed architectural choice for feature extraction may not be optimal for all datasets.\n\nTo address this and explore a more sophisticated approach to feature extraction, I will introduce a **Gated Convolutional Block** as the initial feature extractor. This block utilizes sigmoid gates to control information flow through convolutional layers, allowing the model to dynamically select and combine features. This method, inspired by Gated CNNs, aims to provide a more nuanced and adaptive representation of local sequence motifs, potentially improving overall performance by learning more robust and context-dependent features.\n\nI will use the successful architectural parameters\
    \ from my best model (ID 77) for the subsequent BiLSTM, Additive Attention Pooling, and dense layers, focusing the architectural innovation on the initial CNN stage.\n\nMy next submission will:\n1.  **Define `GatedConvBlock(nn.Module)`**: This module will incorporate two 1D convolutional layers. The first layer's output will be split to form both a main transformation and a gating mechanism (sigmoid-activated), which are then multiplied. A residual connection will be maintained.\n2.  **Update `create_network`**: Replace the `ResidualConvBlock` with this new `GatedConvBlock`.\n\nThis refined feature extraction strategy will allow me to test if dynamic information gating can lead to further improvements in overall model performance across the diverse RNA tasks.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gated CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, gated, hybrid, architecture\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a Gated\
    \ Convolutional Block as the initial feature extractor in the CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling architecture. This block uses sigmoid gates to control information flow through its two 1D convolutional layers, combined with a residual connection. This aims to learn more adaptively weighted and robust local sequence motifs, which are then passed to the BiLSTM and additive attention, striving for enhanced and more balanced performance across all diverse RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits\
    \ = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Gated Convolutional Block with Residual Connection\n  class GatedConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      conv_features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic):\n          # x_input shape: (batch, seq_len, input_features)\n          input_features = x.shape[-1]\n          residual = x\n          \n          #\
    \ First Conv layer, output split for gate and main path\n          conv1_output = nn.Conv(features=2 * self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_gate_1\")(x)\n          \n          # Split into main and gate parts\n          main_path, gate_path = jnp.split(conv1_output, 2, axis=-1)\n          \n          # Apply activation to main path and sigmoid to gate path\n          main_path = nn.relu(main_path)\n          gate_path = nn.sigmoid(gate_path)\n          \n          # Gated activation\n          gated_output = main_path * gate_path\n          gated_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(gated_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Second Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_gate_2\")(gated_output)\n          \n          # Project input features to match conv_features if necessary for residual connection\n          if input_features != self.conv_features:\n\
    \              residual = nn.Dense(features=self.conv_features, name=\"residual_projection\")(residual)\n          \n          # Add residual connection and apply final activation/dropout\n          x = nn.relu(residual + y)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n          return x\n\n  # Main network class\n  class GatedCNNBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. Gated CNN Block for local feature extraction\n          x = GatedConvBlock(\n              conv_features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n\
    \              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n          )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # Output shape: (batch, seq_len, conv_features)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention\
    \ Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Gated CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9, \n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units':\
    \ 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_GatedCNN:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Gated CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = GatedCNNBiLSTMNetwork(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n       \
    \                                dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n\
    \      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_GatedCNN(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Gauging and Refining the CNN-LSTM Interaction**


  Okay, so the "Residual CNN-BiLSTM" model (ID 77) gave us a solid 0.6771, which is definitely progress. The `ncRNA_Accuracy` jump was particularly encouraging, and seeing `CRI-Off_Spearman` improve too suggests the residual block''s initial feature extraction is on the right track. However, the dip in `PRS_R2` tells me we might be hitting a limit with this specific residual structure and hyperparameter combination. It''s time to "question the fundamentals," as the system encourages.


  To really push performance further, I''m going to focus on the interplay between the CNN and LSTM layers. Instead of just a fixed residual CNN block, I''m proposing a **Gated Convolutional Block**. This is inspired by architectures like Gated CNNs for sequence modeling – the core idea is to use sigmoid gates within the convolutional layers to selectively propagate features. This could help the model learn more discriminative representations and potentially mitigate issues like vanishing gradients, without resorting to just increasing the network''s depth or capacity.


  Think of it as adding a smarter, more selective feature filter at the very beginning. I''ll maintain the overall architecture and key parameters from my successful model (ID 77), but I''ll replace the existing residual CNN block with this new gated variant.


  My next submission will have a `GatedConvBlock(nn.Module)` that includes two 1D convolutional layers. Crucially, the first conv layer''s output will be split, with one part going through the "main" transformation and the other through a sigmoid gate. The gated output will then multiply with the transformed output before being added to a residual connection. This ensures feature dimensions align. The subsequent BiLSTM, additive attention pooling, and dense layers will remain the same, with the proven parameters from ID 77.


  Ultimately, I believe this gating mechanism in the initial CNN layers will allow for better feature representations, and subsequently improve performance across all of the RNA-related tasks.

  '
