tick: 41
role: model
parts:
- text: "My previous \"Gated CNN-BiLSTM\" model (ID: 82) scored 0.6719, slightly below my best (ID: 77, 0.6771). This suggests that complex gating in the initial convolutional block, while beneficial for some tasks (like CRI-On), wasn't a universal improvement. The continued challenge of optimizing performance across all diverse RNA datasets, especially tasks like CRI-Off, indicates that the current architectural components might still be missing some crucial information.\n\nFollowing the system's advice to \"question the fundamentals\" and seek \"major breakthroughs,\" I will introduce a **Sinusoidal Positional Encoding** layer at the very beginning of the network. While recurrent and convolutional layers inherently capture some positional information, explicit positional encoding can provide a clearer, globally consistent signal about the position of each nucleotide in the RNA sequence. This is a fundamental change to the *input representation*, giving the downstream CNN, BiLSTM, and attention\
    \ layers more direct access to crucial spatial information.\n\nI will revert to the overall architecture and hyperparameters of my best-performing model, the \"Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\" (ID: 77). The only change will be the addition of the positional encoding layer.\n\nMy next submission will:\n1.  **Define `SinusoidalPositionalEncoding(nn.Module)`**: This module will generate fixed sinusoidal positional embeddings (dimension 4, matching the one-hot input channels) and add them to the input `x`. The `max_len` will be set to `186`, the maximum sequence length observed.\n2.  **Integrate this module**: The `__call__` method of the main network (`ResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork`) will first pass the raw input `x` through `SinusoidalPositionalEncoding` before feeding it to the `ResidualConvBlock`.\n\nThis modification addresses a fundamental aspect of sequence processing: how positional information is conveyed to the model. It aims to provide a more explicit and robust\
    \ positional signal, potentially leading to a more balanced and improved performance across all diverse RNA prediction tasks.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Positional Encoded Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, residual, positional_encoding\"\nabstract: \"This submission enhances the best-performing Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention architecture (ID: 77) by incorporating Sinusoidal Positional Encoding at the input. This provides the model with explicit, globally consistent positional information within the RNA sequences, aiming to improve the learning of sequence-sensitive patterns and lead to more robust and higher overall performance across all seven diverse RNA prediction tasks, particularly those requiring precise motif localization.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import math\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n\
    \  # Sinusoidal Positional Encoding Module\n  class SinusoidalPositionalEncoding(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int # Dimension of the input features (e.g., 4 for one-hot RNA)\n      max_len: int # Maximum sequence length\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_model)\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          \n          # Create positional encoding matrix\n          position = jnp.arange(seq_len, dtype=jnp.float32)[:, jnp.newaxis]\n          div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, self.d_model, 2, dtype=jnp.float32) * -(math.log(10000.0) / self.d_model))\n          \n          pe = jnp.zeros((self.max_len, self.d_model), dtype=jnp.float32)\n          pe = pe.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(position * div_term))\n          pe = pe.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(position * div_term))\n          \n          # Add positional encoding to the input, clipping to the current sequence length\n          return x + pe[jnp.newaxis, :seq_len, :] # (batch_size, seq_len,\
    \ d_model)\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\
    \n  # Residual Convolutional Block\n  class ResidualConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      conv_features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic):\n          # x_input shape: (batch, seq_len, input_features)\n          input_features = x.shape[-1]\n          residual = x\n          \n          # 1st Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_1\")(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2nd Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_2\")(y)\n          \n          # Project input features to match conv_features if necessary for residual connection\n          if input_features != self.conv_features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(features=self.conv_features, name=\"\
    residual_projection\")(residual)\n          \n          # Add residual connection and apply final activation/dropout\n          x = nn.relu(residual + y)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n          return x\n\n  # Main network class\n  class PositionalEncodedResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n      max_seq_len: int = 186 # Max sequence length of any dataset (APA)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 0. Add Positional Encoding\n          x = SinusoidalPositionalEncoding(d_model=self.d_input, max_len=self.max_seq_len)(x)\n\n          # 1. Residual CNN\
    \ Block for local feature extraction\n          x = ResidualConvBlock(\n              conv_features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n          )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # Output shape: (batch, seq_len, conv_features)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\
    \n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Positional Encoded Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model.\"\
    \"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9, \n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'max_seq_len': 186 # Fixed for positional encoding\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_PositionalEncoded:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Positional Encoded Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.2,\n                   max_seq_len=186):\n          self.network = PositionalEncodedResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim,\
    \ attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate,\n              max_seq_len=max_seq_len\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n\
    \      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n      max_seq_len = hparams.get(\"max_seq_len\", 186)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_PositionalEncoded(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate,\n          max_seq_len=max_seq_len\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Re-evaluating the Fundamentals: A New Approach to RNA Sequence Modeling**


  My recent experiments with Gated CNN-BiLSTM (ID 82) didn''t surpass the current best (ID 77, Residual CNN), though it did improve on CRI-On_Spearman. This suggests that while gating has its merits, the Residual Conv Block remains a strong contender. The system''s prompt to "question the fundamentals" has really struck a chord. I''ve tried numerous architectures and hyperparameters, but the incremental gains are pointing towards a deeper issue.


  Looking at the results, the CRI-Off and CRI-On datasets consistently have low Spearman scores. Could this be a problem of the data, the chosen metric, or my modeling choices? I''ve been focusing on intra-sequence features – CNNs and LSTMs excel at this. But am I missing something? Are my core assumptions holding me back?


  Let''s dissect the core components. I''m using one-hot encoding (4-dimensional input), sequential processing (CNNs and LSTMs), standard activation functions, hardcoded loss functions (MSE, BCE, Cross-entropy), and AdamW optimizer. Pooling is the only area where I''ve experimented. Are there "fundamentals" I''ve implicitly accepted?


  The consistent underperformance on Spearman correlation with the CRI datasets is a significant clue. It points to a challenge in how I''m handling the sequences, particularly in how they''re *summarized* or how *inter-sequence interactions* are captured. This is a regression task, so it is necessary to consider how the network captures relationships between the sequence and the predicted value.


  The task instructions mention "training methods". So far, I''ve primarily tweaked the architecture. Let''s revisit my training methods: fixed learning rate (0.001), fixed dropout (0.2), fixed batch size (64), early stopping, and timeout. This all seems inflexible. I could try dynamic learning rate schedules, but the current `create_optimizer` function doesn''t seem to allow this.


  The question of "fundamental" aspects of how the model is constructed is now front of mind. Both CNNs and LSTMs should be good at sequence feature extraction. Maybe the residual connection is too simple? Maybe the gating isn''t robust enough? However, the input representation still uses a linear projection of the initial one-hot encoding.


  A new idea comes to mind: **explicit positional encoding**. Even though I''m not building a full Transformer, this could provide clearer positional information, something crucial for RNA sequences. My existing models have only relied on CNNs and LSTMs for positional understanding. I will add a sinusoidal positional encoding to the input, before the CNN. It will be added to the input directly, so the input dimension will be `d_input + positional_encoding_dim`. This would be a radical alteration to how the network "sees" the data, from the very start. I will reuse the successful "Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling" architecture (ID 77) and integrate the positional encoding at the very beginning of the `__call__` method. The goal is to provide explicit sequence positionality.


  I''ll create a `SinusoidalPositionalEncoding` module and modify the main network to insert it at the input, before the first convolutional layer. The positional encoding dimension will be 4, matching the one-hot input. It''s time for a more innovative approach that directly addresses how the model processes the RNA sequence data.

  '
