thinking_content: "**My Current Thinking**\n\nOkay, so the `SinusoidalPositionalEncoding` fix, submission ID 87, is finally running. The previous attempt (ID 85) failed, but this one should be good – it dynamically generates the positional encoding matrix based on sequence length, solving that pesky shape mismatch.  Now it's a waiting game.\n\nI'm focused on what comes next. Right now, this ID 87 is the `SinusoidalPositionalEncoding` integrated into my best-performing architecture so far (ID 77: Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling), and I'm hoping explicit positional information will give the model an edge, especially on those trickier datasets like CRI-Off and PRS. My current best score is 0.6771 (from ID 77), so any improvement is welcome.\n\nOnce the results are in, here's the plan:\n\n1.  **Analyze the heck out of ID 87.** I need to see if it improved the overall score. Did it outperform ID 77? I'll also dive into the per-dataset performance – did it help with the tasks\
  \ where previous models faltered?\n2.  **Make a decision based on the results.**\n    *   **If ID 87 rocks:** I'll start with some hyperparameter tuning (learning rate, dropout, maybe tweak the encoding itself).\n    *   **If ID 87 bombs:**  It might be that explicit positional encoding isn't the key here, or that it's not meshing well with the CNN-BiLSTM. I'd then consider:\n        *   Going back to the trusty ID 77 and working on something else.\n        *   Trying a different type of positional embedding (maybe learnable ones?).\n        *   Or, I might need to consider more fundamental architectural changes to how information flows.\n\nThe system reminds me I can multitask, but given this critical evaluation, focusing on this experiment seems like the most effective use of this turn. I will wait for the results.\n"
