tick: 45
role: model
parts:
- text: "My recent attempt with positional encoding (ID: 87) yielded a lower score than my previous best (ID: 77), indicating that not all additional complexities improve overall performance across diverse tasks. This highlights the challenge of finding a universally optimal architecture.\n\nMy current highest-scoring model is ID: 77, \"Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\" (0.6771). I will return to this architecture, which uses a two-layer residual convolutional block for initial feature extraction. To further explore the potential of this initial feature extraction stage, I will deepen the `ResidualConvBlock` by adding a third convolutional layer within it. This aims to allow the CNN to learn even richer and more hierarchical local features before the sequence is processed by the BiLSTM and additive attention mechanism. This is a focused refinement to a proven component of my best model.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Deeper Residual CNN-BiLSTM with\
    \ Additive Attention Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, residual, deep, architecture\"\nabstract: \"This submission deepens the initial feature extraction in the best-performing Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention architecture (ID: 77). The Residual Convolutional Block now incorporates three 1D convolutional layers, designed to learn more complex and hierarchical local sequence motifs. These enhanced features are then passed to the BiLSTM and additive attention pooling, aiming for further improvements in overall performance across all diverse RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n      \
    \    # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Deeper Residual Convolutional Block\n  class DeeperResidualConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      conv_features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic):\n          # x_input shape:\
    \ (batch, seq_len, input_features)\n          input_features = x.shape[-1]\n          residual = x\n          \n          # 1st Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_1\")(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2nd Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_2\")(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # 3rd Conv layer (added)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_3\")(y)\n          \n          # Project input features to match conv_features if necessary for residual connection\n          if input_features != self.conv_features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(features=self.conv_features,\
    \ name=\"residual_projection\")(residual)\n          \n          # Add residual connection and apply final activation/dropout\n          x = nn.relu(residual + y)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n          return x\n\n  # Main network class\n  class DeeperResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. Deeper Residual CNN Block for local feature extraction\n          x = DeeperResidualConvBlock( # Changed to deeper block\n              conv_features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n       \
    \       dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n          )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # Output shape: (batch, seq_len, conv_features)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention\
    \ Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition (same as ID 77)\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Deeper Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9, \n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n  \
    \        'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_DeeperResidualCNN:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Deeper Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.2):\n          self.network = DeeperResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params':\
    \ params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n  \
    \    dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.2)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_DeeperResidualCNN(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Focusing on Refinement: Deeper Residual Blocks**


  Alright, let''s break this down. That last positional encoding experiment (ID 87) really didn''t pan out as expected. While it nudged up a couple of metrics, the overall score dipped significantly below my previous bests (IDs 77 and 51). This reinforces the "Be Strategic" guideline – sometimes more complexity isn''t the answer, and adding generic features can cause unwanted trade-offs. It''s time to step back from chasing the bleeding edge and dig deeper into what *already* works.


  Looking back, the "Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling" (ID 77) has proven itself as the clear winner. The residual block seems to be the star here. My exploration of various architectural configurations – from simple CNNs and BiLSTMs to more complex hybrids with attention – has shown that adding more bells and whistles doesn''t guarantee a superior outcome. It''s often about finding the right balance.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m going to follow the "Be Persistent" guideline and refine what''s already successful. Instead of throwing more complex features at the problem, I''m going to enhance the depth of the proven residual block within my best architecture (ID 77). Currently, the residual block has two convolutional layers. I''ll increase this to three convolutional layers while keeping the rest of the architecture (BiLSTM, Additive Attention, hyperparameters, etc.) the same as in ID 77. This controlled experiment will allow me to see if extracting deeper, more hierarchical features locally, before sending it to the rest of the model, is the key to unlocking the next level of performance.


  To summarize, my next submission will:


  1.  **Modify the `ResidualConvBlock`**: Increase convolutional layers within the block from 2 to 3, keeping `conv_features` the same.

  2.  **Keep the rest of the `PositionalEncodedResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork` structure and hyperparameters from ID 77** (reverting to a kernel size of 9 and removing the detrimental positional encoding).


  This is a focused refinement on the core CNN feature extractor of my best performing model.

  '
