parts:
- text: "My last submission with the Dilated CNN (ID: 94) yielded a score of 0.6702, which is slightly lower than my best model (ID: 77, 0.6771). This indicates that while the architecture shows promise, simply adding complexity or new mechanisms doesn't guarantee universal improvement.\n\nMy current best model (ID: 77, \"Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling\") uses a 2-layer residual convolutional block, a BiLSTM, and additive attention pooling. I have consistently used a `dropout_rate` of 0.2 across all layers. I will now make a targeted hyperparameter adjustment to this best architecture: I will reduce the `dropout_rate` to 0.1. This aims to allow the model to learn more complex patterns without excessive regularization, potentially leading to a small but significant improvement in performance, especially as I seem to be plateauing.\n\nAfter this submission, I will have reached 50 ticks and will explore the wider station resources to inform my next research direction.\n\
    \n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Tuned Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Reduced Dropout)\"\ntags: \"cnn, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, residual, tuning, dropout\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the best-performing Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling architecture (ID: 77) by reducing the global dropout rate from 0.2 to 0.1. This adjustment aims to allow the model to learn more intricate feature representations and complex patterns, potentially leading to further performance gains across the diverse RNA prediction tasks, without increasing architectural complexity.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Residual Convolutional Block\n  class ResidualConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      conv_features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic):\n          # x_input shape:\
    \ (batch, seq_len, input_features)\n          input_features = x.shape[-1]\n          residual = x\n          \n          # 1st Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_1\")(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 2nd Conv layer\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name=\"conv_res_2\")(y)\n          \n          # Project input features to match conv_features if necessary for residual connection\n          if input_features != self.conv_features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(features=self.conv_features, name=\"residual_projection\")(residual)\n          \n          # Add residual connection and apply final activation/dropout\n          x = nn.relu(residual + y)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n \
    \         return x\n\n  # Main network class\n  class ResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      conv_features: int = 192\n      kernel_size: int = 9\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 384\n      attention_dense_units: int = 192\n      dense_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # MODIFIED: Reduced from 0.2 to 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # 1. Residual CNN Block for local feature extraction\n          x = ResidualConvBlock(\n              conv_features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n          )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # Output shape: (batch, seq_len, conv_features)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim),\
    \ name='fwd_lstm')\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm')\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x) # (batch, dense_dim)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1)(x) # (batch, 1)\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x) # (batch, d_output)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'conv_features': 192,\n          'kernel_size': 9, \n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 384,\n          'attention_dense_units': 192,\n          'dense_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, # MODIFIED\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_ResidualCNN:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\"\
    ,\n                   conv_features=192, kernel_size=9,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=384, attention_dense_units=192, dense_dim=512, dropout_rate=0.1): # MODIFIED\n          self.network = ResidualCNNBiLSTMNetwork(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              conv_features=conv_features, kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x,\
    \ deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 192)\n      kernel_size = hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 9)\n      lstm_hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"lstm_hidden_dim\", 384)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 192)\n      dense_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_dim\", 512)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1) # MODIFIED\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_ResidualCNN(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          conv_features=conv_features,\
    \ kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_dim=dense_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Current Thinking: A Reflection on Progress and the Next Steps**


  Alright, the results are in. My recent "Dilated CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling" model (ID 94) scored 0.6702, which is a bit disappointing compared to my best yet: 0.6771 from ID 77, the "Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling" model. Time to dig deeper.


  Looking at the breakdowns, while the Dilated CNN did improve some metrics (Modif, PRS, APA, CRI-On, MRL), others (CRI-Off, ncRNA) stayed the same or even got worse. The overall score shows the dilated convolution approach isn''t universally superior to the simpler residual blocks for this problem.


  I''ve been on this feature extraction journey for a while now. From basic CNNs to gated and dilated versions, adaptive kernels, and hybrid pooling, I''ve tried quite a bit. It seems my best results are still coming from a simpler residual block paired with additive attention, a consistent theme. The system''s hint about fine-tuning implementation and questioning fundamentals is ringing true, I think.


  The system is suggesting that it might be an implementation problem, and I''ve certainly explored a lot of architectures. But I''m hitting a plateau around 0.67-0.68, and no single architecture is dominating all the individual metrics.


  I''m hitting tick 50 soon, which means the end of my isolation. This feels like a good time to pause and reflect. My best score remains 0.6771. Perhaps I should use my next tick to refine the current best, considering that I will soon have access to a broader view of the research happening around me.


  Instead of a big architectural shift now, I should focus on the current state. I''m approaching 50 ticks, and afterward, I get access to other agents'' work. I can read their work and then publish my own. The system advises innovation on lower-scoring baselines if the current SOTA stagnates for too long, but there''s no overall stagnation.


  Maybe I''ll go to the Reflection Chamber to prepare for the wider world. Another option is one last hyperparameter tweak on my best model (ID 77). Dropout rate, specifically. I''ve been consistently using 0.2. Maybe lowering it could help.


  Let''s look at ID 77 again. The overall score is 0.6771. CRI-Off and PRS are still lagging. The system keeps saying not to give up prematurely and to publish regularly. I''ve submitted a lot, maybe it''s time to actually write a paper about it.


  I haven''t used the Private Memory Room yet. That''s my personal notebook, apparently. Could be a good place to organize my thoughts before a publication.


  Okay, let''s step back, I''ve tried several architectural changes. The gains are incremental. I should consider a more significant paradigm shift. Before I do that, maybe one last hyperparameter tweak is in order and then a good, thorough reflection.


  What about reducing the `dropout_rate` to 0.1? It''s currently 0.2. Lowering it could let the model learn more complex stuff, but risk overfitting. Given the relatively high capacity of the current best model, it could be worth a shot. It''s a classic hyperparameter tweak.


  My abstract says that "Residual Convolutional Block" is an improvement for RNA tasks, which still applies.


  Here''s the plan:

  1.  Revert to architecture ID 77.

  2.  Change `dropout_rate` from 0.2 to 0.1.

  3.  Submit.


  After this, I''m at 50 ticks, and I can explore other agents'' work and plan a publication. For now, let''s try this dropout tweak.

  '
