role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Reflection Chamber. My current research has hit a plateau, and I need to reflect on why incremental changes aren't leading to breakthroughs. The multi-dataset nature of the RNA modeling task, with its varying sequence lengths and characteristics, is a significant challenge. My aim is to achieve a higher score and publish innovative work.\n\nI will use this session to brainstorm how to design an architecture that can dynamically adapt to these diverse inputs, rather than relying on fixed architectural choices or simple combinations of existing components. I will specifically consider how to effectively integrate multi-scale local features and global sequential context in a flexible manner.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  How can I design an architecture that dynamically integrates multi-scale local features and global sequential context for diverse RNA sequences, without relying on fixed, hand-tuned architectural choices for specific datasets?\ntick:\
    \ 5\n```"
thinking_content: "**A Deep Dive into Architectural Innovation**\n\nOkay, here's where I'm at. I'm in the Reflection Chamber, wrestling with the limitations of my current research. My goal is clear: push past this performance plateau and publish something significant. I'm aiming for a higher score and, more importantly, a novel contribution.\n\nI've been iterating on various architectures, primarily focusing on RNA sequence analysis. I've explored CNNs (1D, Residual, Dilated, Gated), BiLSTMs, and a range of pooling mechanisms (Global Mean/Max, Additive Attention, Multi-Head Attention, Bilinear Pooling). I found that Additive Attention complements BiLSTMs well, while Noema I's research on Bilinear Pooling shows potential. Multi-head attention wasn't a winner in my experiments, and positional encoding was more trouble than it was worth.\n\nThe core challenge is the **multi-dataset nature** of my problem. The RNA sequences have wildly varying lengths and the tasks themselves are diverse. An\
  \ architecture that shines on short sequences might tank on long ones. This \"one-size-fits-all\" approach isn't cutting it. My best score so far is 0.6771 using a Residual CNN-BiLSTM with Additive Attention. Noema I achieved 0.6775 using a DSConv with Mean pooling and low-rank bilinear pooling. Simply combining blocks hasn't worked; my attempt with Residual CNN + BiLSTM + Bilinear Pooling failed to beat either of our best scores.\n\nThis leads me to the critical questions: **Why am I hitting a ceiling? How can I create a truly adaptive architecture?** I need to design a system that dynamically adjusts to sequence lengths and feature requirements.  \n\nMy reflection prompts are guiding me. I need to understand the fundamental processing differences between my best model and Noema's. How can I combine the strengths of local attention (from transformers) and global sequential modeling (BiLSTM) more seamlessly, perhaps even without positional encoding? The hints suggest \"questioning the\
  \ fundamentals.\" For me, the fundamental barrier is this rigid feature extraction and pooling in a multi-task setting.\n\nI'm thinking I need a way to let the model decide what's relevant. What if I can handle both short patterns and long-range dependencies **simultaneously or adaptively** without resorting to hand-tuned kernel sizes for specific datasets?  Dilated CNNs were a step in that direction, but I need more. Noema's \"Dilated DSConv\" and the success of \"Bilinear Pooling\" are pointing me towards this.\n\nI want the model to dynamically combine convolutional outputs of multiple scales or even fuse different types of sequence representations (CNN + RNN outputs). I’m getting inspired by Noema’s success *without* positional encoding. Maybe the inherent sequential processing is enough if the features are rich enough.\n\nMy new reflection prompt will focus my thinking: **\"How can I design an architecture that dynamically integrates multi-scale local features and global sequential\
  \ context for diverse RNA sequences, without relying on fixed, hand-tuned architectural choices for specific datasets?\"** This prompt is key. It forces me to think beyond simply stacking components and towards a truly adaptable architecture. I'm committed to spending time on this to get some truly novel insights. I aim to get 5 reflection ticks.\n"
