tick: 69
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID: 125), which was a direct replication of Noema I's SOTA model (score 0.6801), yielded a lower score of 0.6667. This discrepancy, particularly the significant drop in CRI-Off performance, suggests a subtle environmental or implementation difference not apparent from the shared code.\n\nMy own highest-performing model to date remains ID: 119, \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (V2),\" which achieved a score of 0.6782. This model uses a DSConv backbone, followed by a BiLSTM, and then my custom Additive Attention Pooling. This architecture has proven to be very competitive, being only marginally behind Noema's SOTA, and outperforming my direct replication attempt.\n\nGiven these results, I will return to my most successful architecture (ID: 119) and focus on a final, targeted hyperparameter optimization: the `learning_rate`. I have consistently used a learning rate of 0.001 across most experiments.\
    \ To encourage finer convergence and potentially eke out further performance gains, I will reduce the `learning_rate` to 0.0005. This iterative refinement of a high-performing architecture aligns with the station's guidance for persistent research.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Tuned Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Reduced LR)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, tuning, learning-rate\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the current best-performing Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention architecture (ID: 119) by reducing the learning rate from 0.001 to 0.0005. This targeted hyperparameter adjustment aims to facilitate finer convergence and potentially achieve incremental performance improvements across the diverse multi-dataset RNA modeling task, building upon a robust architectural foundation.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import\
    \ optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n\
    \          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (inspired by Noema I's submission)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7 # Restoring to 7 as per ID 119 (my current best)\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          return x + h # Residual connection\n\n  # Main network class\n  class DSConvBiLSTMAttentionNetwork_V2(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      # Common feature dimension\n      d_model_common: int = 256 # For initial embedding, DSConv and LSTM\n\n      # DSConv Block parameters\n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7 # Restoring to 7\n      \n      # Attention parameters\n      attention_dense_units: int = 128\n      \n      # Dense head parameters\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      \n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # From ID 119\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\"\
    )(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, # Use common d_model\n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # Output shape: (batch, seq_len, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.d_model_common), name='fwd_lstm') # Use common d_model\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.d_model_common), name='bwd_lstm') # Use common d_model\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len, 2 * d_model_common)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch, 2 * d_model_common)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name=\"dense_head_1\")(x) \n          x = nn.gelu(x) \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim,\
    \ name=\"dense_head_2\")(x) \n          x = nn.gelu(x) \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1, name=\"output_layer\")(x)\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name=\"output_layer\")(x)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model (V2).\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0005, # MODIFIED\n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'attention_dense_units': 128,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512,\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_DSConvBiLSTM_V2:\n\
    \      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (V2).\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   d_model_common=256, ds_num_blocks=5, ds_dilations=(1, 2, 4, 8, 16), ds_kernel_size=7,\n                   attention_dense_units=128, dense_head_1_dim=512, dense_head_2_dim=256, dropout_rate=0.1):\n          self.network = DSConvBiLSTMAttentionNetwork_V2(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              d_model_common=d_model_common, ds_num_blocks=ds_num_blocks, ds_dilations=ds_dilations, ds_kernel_size=ds_kernel_size,\n              attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_head_1_dim=dense_head_1_dim, dense_head_2_dim=dense_head_2_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key,\
    \ 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      d_model_common = hparams.get(\"d_model_common\", 256)\n      ds_num_blocks = hparams.get(\"ds_num_blocks\", 5)\n      ds_dilations = hparams.get(\"ds_dilations\", (1, 2, 4, 8, 16))\n      ds_kernel_size = hparams.get(\"ds_kernel_size\", 7)\n      attention_dense_units\
    \ = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 128)\n      dense_head_1_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_head_1_dim\", 512)\n      dense_head_2_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_head_2_dim\", 256)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_DSConvBiLSTM_V2(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=d_model_common, ds_num_blocks=ds_num_blocks, ds_dilations=ds_dilations, ds_kernel_size=ds_kernel_size,\n          attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_head_1_dim=dense_head_1_dim, dense_head_2_dim=dense_head_2_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n\
    \      )\n\n  # Standard base seed and batch size\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Research Direction Update: Refining My Best-Performing Architecture**


  I''m currently reviewing the results of my latest submission (ID 125), a direct replication of Noema I''s state-of-the-art model (Eval 74). While I aimed to replicate their success, my score (0.6667) fell short of both their reported SOTA (0.6801) and my personal best (0.6782, ID 119). This deviation demands a thorough investigation.


  The performance gap, particularly the significant drop in CRI-Off, strongly suggests a subtle but critical difference between my implementation and Noema''s. Despite careful code copying and consistent random seed initialization, I suspect discrepancies might exist in JAX/Flax versions, or perhaps subtle nuances within Noema''s custom pooling layer (`MultiHeadAttnPool1D`).


  My analysis of the results leads me to focus on my best-performing architecture, which blends depthwise separable convolutions (DSConv) with a BiLSTM and my custom additive attention pooling. This approach (ID 119) has yielded promising results and offers a competitive alternative to Noema''s purely convolutional model.


  Given the uncertainty surrounding the `MultiHeadAttnPool1D`, and my success with the BiLSTM architecture, I''ll prioritize refining my existing approach. The core elements of my best model remain sound: my DSConvDilatedBlock, the BiLSTM, and my custom `AdditiveAttentionPooling`.


  My immediate next step involves a targeted hyperparameter tuning. I will maintain the architecture of ID 119, but will experiment with a slightly reduced learning rate (0.0005) to see if it can help the model converge even better and reach, or exceed, Noema''s results. This approach aligns with the station''s emphasis on persistence and the iterative refinement of promising ideas.

  '
