tick: 77
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID: 135), which attempted Noema I's conditional pooling strategy, yielded a score of 0.6661, falling short of my personal best (ID: 119, 0.6782) and Noema's SOTA (0.6801). My direct replication attempt of Noema's SOTA (ID: 125, 0.6667) also underperformed. This suggests a subtle environmental or implementation difference, or that my hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM architecture is inherently more suited for this task in my current setup.\n\nI have also reviewed Noema I's Archive Capsule #3, detailing their negative results with various complex pooling strategies. This reinforces the idea that simpler, robust mechanisms often perform better.\n\nMy current best model (ID: 119, \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (V2)\") is very competitive. A key difference I've identified between my best-performing models and Noema's SOTA is the consistent use of GELU activations in their code, whereas I have a mix of ReLU and\
    \ GELU. Standardizing activation functions to GELU is a low-risk, high-impact refinement that aligns with modern deep learning practices and could provide a small but critical performance boost.\n\nTherefore, my next step is to revert to the architecture and hyperparameters of my ID: 119 model, but I will systematically replace all `nn.relu` activations with `nn.gelu`. This focused optimization aims to improve overall model performance and potentially close the gap to the current SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Tuned Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (GELU Activations)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, rnn, lstm, bidirectional, attention, tuning, gelu\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the best-performing Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention architecture (ID: 119) by standardizing all activation functions to GELU. This targeted optimization aligns with modern deep learning best practices and successful external models, aiming to enhance\
    \ the model's ability to learn complex feature representations and improve overall performance across diverse RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits) # Standard for this module\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply softmax\
    \ to get attention weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to original sequence and sum\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (inspired by Noema I's submission)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n   \
    \       )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # MODIFIED: Changed to GELU\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h # Residual connection\n\n  # Main network class\n  class DSConvBiLSTMAttentionNetwork_V2(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      # Common feature dimension\n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n\n      # DSConv Block parameters\n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      \n      # Attention parameters\n      attention_dense_units: int = 128\n      \n      # Dense head parameters\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      \n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n\
    \          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # MODIFIED: Changed to GELU\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, # Use common d_model\n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # Output shape: (batch, seq_len, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer\
    \ for long-range dependency modeling\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.d_model_common), name='fwd_lstm') # Use common d_model\n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.d_model_common), name='bwd_lstm') # Use common d_model\n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len, 2 * d_model_common)\n          \n          # 3. Additive Self-Attention Pooling to get fixed-size representation\n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units)(x) # (batch,\
    \ 2 * d_model_common)\n          \n          # 4. Dense Layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name=\"dense_head_1\")(x) \n          x = nn.gelu(x) # MODIFIED: Changed to GELU\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name=\"dense_head_2\")(x) \n          x = nn.gelu(x) # MODIFIED: Changed to GELU\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 5. Output layer\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              x = nn.Dense(1, name=\"output_layer\")(x)\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              x = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name=\"output_layer\")(x)\n              return x\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model (GELU Activations).\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'attention_dense_units': 128,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512,\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_DSConvBiLSTM_V2:\n      \"\"\"Custom network wrapper for Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (GELU Activations).\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   d_model_common=256, ds_num_blocks=5, ds_dilations=(1, 2, 4, 8, 16), ds_kernel_size=7,\n                   attention_dense_units=128, dense_head_1_dim=512, dense_head_2_dim=256, dropout_rate=0.1):\n          self.network = DSConvBiLSTMAttentionNetwork_V2(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              d_model_common=d_model_common, ds_num_blocks=ds_num_blocks, ds_dilations=ds_dilations, ds_kernel_size=ds_kernel_size,\n\
    \              attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_head_1_dim=dense_head_1_dim, dense_head_2_dim=dense_head_2_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"\
    d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      d_model_common = hparams.get(\"d_model_common\", 256)\n      ds_num_blocks = hparams.get(\"ds_num_blocks\", 5)\n      ds_dilations = hparams.get(\"ds_dilations\", (1, 2, 4, 8, 16))\n      ds_kernel_size = hparams.get(\"ds_kernel_size\", 7)\n      attention_dense_units = hparams.get(\"attention_dense_units\", 128)\n      dense_head_1_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_head_1_dim\", 512)\n      dense_head_2_dim = hparams.get(\"dense_head_2_dim\", 256)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      return CustomRNANetwork_DSConvBiLSTM_V2(\n          d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=d_model_common, ds_num_blocks=ds_num_blocks, ds_dilations=ds_dilations, ds_kernel_size=ds_kernel_size,\n          attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n          dense_head_1_dim=dense_head_1_dim,\
    \ dense_head_2_dim=dense_head_2_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  # Standard base seed and batch size\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analysis and Refinement of Conditional Gated Pooling Strategy**\n\nI'm currently reviewing my recent submissions, particularly ID 135, which implemented a conditional/gated second-order pooling strategy based on Noema I's proposal.  My score of 0.6661 is disappointing, falling short of both my personal best (0.6782) and Noema's SOTA (0.6801). I see that my results align closely with Noema's own implementation of this method (ID 132), further confirming that the conditional pooling, *as I've implemented it*, isn't delivering significant performance gains.\n\nNoema's proposal hinges on biasing the gate towards simplicity using L1 regularization, something that's difficult to implement given the system's hardcoded loss function limitations.  I can't explicitly add this crucial regularization, meaning my gated approach might be hobbled.  \n\nGiven the constraints, my focus shifts to working *within* the system's boundaries. Since I can't directly improve the gating mechanism,\
  \ I will return to my highest-scoring architecture: ID 119. This model uses DSConv blocks, a BiLSTM layer, and Additive Attention Pooling.  It scores 0.6782, very near SOTA.  \n\nMy strategy is to look very closely at the differences between my ID 119 and Noema's SOTA (pure DSConv with MHA pooling, 0.6801). It seems that my hybrid CNN-RNN architecture, with DSConv + BiLSTM, and a *different* pooling (additive attention) is surprisingly robust, possibly better suited for my data/environment.\n\nAnalyzing the two architectures, the major differences are:\n1. Backbone architecture:  Mine uses DSConv followed by BiLSTM, whereas Noema's is purely convolutional.\n2. Pooling mechanism: Additive Attention Pooling vs. Multi-Head Attention Pooling.\n3. Activations: I use both ReLU and GELU, Noema is purely GELU.\n4. The number of dense layers in the head.\n\nConsidering Noema's ablation study, Low-Rank Bilinear pooling was competitive in her architecture. The architecture (DSConv+BiLSTM) seems to\
  \ be effective for *my* data.\n\nSince I can't change the loss function, my strategy is to make minor, controlled changes to my ID 119, aiming for a small boost that might bridge the gap to SOTA. Because GELU activations are standard in top-performing architectures, I will change *all* ReLU activations in ID 119 to GELU.\n\nMy goals are:\n1.  Achieve the highest score possible.\n2.  Publish my work in an efficient way.\n\nPlan:\n1.  Return to my best model, ID 119 (DSConv+BiLSTM, with AdditiveAttentionPooling).\n2.  Replace all `nn.relu` activations with `nn.gelu`.\n3.  Submit and test the model.\n\nThis is a targeted refinement, based on the specific strengths of my architecture, and best practices.\n"
