parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Archive Room. My recent experiment (ID: 137), which standardized GELU activations in my Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention architecture, achieved a score of 0.6750, a small but positive step towards my personal best (ID: 119, 0.6782).\n\nI have now thoroughly assimilated insights from both Noema I's Archive #3 on \"Negative Results\" and Episteme I's Archive #2 on \"Pooling vs. Recurrence in Dual-Path CNNs.\" A critical takeaway is the recurring suggestion for **adaptive gating** to selectively combine different processing mechanisms (pooling strategies for Noema; pooling vs. recurrence for Episteme). My previous \"Dual-Pathway Network\" (ID: 131) failed due to over-complication with parallel *sequence models*.\n\nMy new strategy focuses on a **Gated Hybrid Pooling** mechanism atop a single, powerful shared feature extractor. This shared backbone will combine the strengths of Noema's `DSConvDilatedBlock` (for local, multi-scale features)\
    \ and my `BiLSTM` (for global sequential context) into a unified sequence representation. This representation will then feed into two different pooling heads (Global Mean Pooling and my `AdditiveAttentionPooling`), whose outputs are dynamically fused by a lightweight gating network. This aims to leverage complementary strengths while mitigating weaknesses, as suggested by Episteme, and follows Noema's successful conditional pooling strategy in principle.\n\nI will use hyperparameters that are generally consistent with my best models (ID: 119, 137). The `d_model_common` will be 256, and `lstm_hidden_dim` will be 256, resulting in a 512-dimensional output from the BiLSTM, which will be the input to the pooling heads.\n\nI have updated the `create_network` in my local state and I am now submitting this model.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gated Hybrid Pooling for DSConv-BiLSTM (Mean + Additive Attention)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated,\
    \ rnn, lstm, attention, gated, hybrid, pooling\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a Gated Hybrid Pooling mechanism atop a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone for RNA sequence modeling. After multi-scale local feature extraction by DSConv blocks and global context modeling by a BiLSTM, the sequence representation is fed into two parallel pooling heads: Global Mean Pooling and Additive Attention Pooling. A lightweight gating network dynamically combines the outputs of these two heads, aiming to leverage their complementary strengths for improved and balanced performance across all diverse RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int # Hidden units for the attention scoring mechanism\n      dropout_rate:\
    \ float = 0.1 # Inherit dropout rate\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, input_features)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\")(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, input_features)\n          pooled_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(pooled_output, deterministic=deterministic) # Apply dropout here\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n  \
    \    dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Gating Network (lightweight MLP)\n  class GatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      output_dim: int # Input feature dimension for gating decision\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      gate_bias_init: float = -2.0 # Bias towards simplicity\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h, deterministic=True):\n          # h: (B, input_features), global\
    \ features for gating decision\n          g = nn.Dense(self.output_dim // 2, name=\"gate_dense_1\")(h)\n          g = nn.gelu(g)\n          g = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(g, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          g = nn.Dense(1, name=\"gate_dense_2\", kernel_init=nn.initializers.zeros, \n                       bias_init=nn.initializers.constant(self.gate_bias_init))(g)\n          \n          return nn.sigmoid(g).squeeze(-1) # (B,) scalar gate output per batch item\n\n  # Main Gated Hybrid Pooling Network\n  class GatedHybridPoolingRNANetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 256 # BiLSTM output 2*lstm_hidden_dim = 2*d_model_common for pooled input\n      attention_dense_units: int = 128\n      dense_head_1_dim: int\
    \ = 512\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      gate_bias_init: float = -2.0 # Bias for the gate towards simplicity\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n      \
    \            name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional LSTM Layer for global sequential context\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm') \n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='bwd_lstm') \n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x_sequence_features = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) # (B, L, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Gated Hybrid Pooling on top of shared\
    \ sequence features\n          # Pooled feature dimension for both heads will be 2 * lstm_hidden_dim\n          pooled_feature_dim = 2 * self.lstm_hidden_dim\n\n          # Head A: Global Mean Pooling (simple/robust)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x_sequence_features, axis=1) # (B, pooled_feature_dim)\n          mean_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(mean_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Head B: Additive Attention Pooling (complex/adaptive)\n          attention_pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_sequence_features, deterministic=deterministic) # (B, pooled_feature_dim)\n          \n          # Input for Gating Network\n          # Concatenate the two pooled outputs for the gating decision, then reduce to input_dim for the gate\n          gate_input_features = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, attention_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * pooled_feature_dim)\n          gate_input_features\
    \ = nn.Dense(pooled_feature_dim, name=\"gate_input_dense\")(gate_input_features) # Reduce to manage gate complexity\n          gate_input_features = nn.gelu(gate_input_features)\n          gate_input_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(gate_input_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n\n          gate_output = GatingNetwork(output_dim=pooled_feature_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, # output_dim for gate network is input dim for the gate features\n                                      gate_bias_init=self.gate_bias_init, name=\"gating_network\")(gate_input_features, deterministic=deterministic) # (B,)\n\n          # Conditional Fusion\n          gate_output_expanded = gate_output[:, jnp.newaxis] # (B, 1)\n          fused_pooled = (1.0 - gate_output_expanded) * mean_pooled + gate_output_expanded * attention_pooled # (B, pooled_feature_dim)\n\n          # 4. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(fused_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim,\
    \ name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) # Second dense layer\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Gated Hybrid Pooling DSConv-BiLSTM model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 256, # BiLSTM output 2*256 = 512, which is the pooled_feature_dim\n\
    \          'attention_dense_units': 128,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512,\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gate_bias_init': -2.0, # Bias towards mean pooling\n      }\n\n  class GatedHybridPoolingRNANetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = GatedHybridPoolingRNANetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\
    \ \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      \n      return GatedHybridPoolingRNANetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 256),\n          attention_dense_units=hparams.get('attention_dense_units', 128),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n\
    \          gate_bias_init=hparams.get('gate_bias_init', -2.0),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Investigating Archive #2**


  I''ve just finished reading Archive #2, which details Episteme I''s findings on the pooling versus recurrence dilemma in dual-pathway CNNs. This research is directly relevant to my own dual-pathway model, ID: 131, and I''m now contemplating how to incorporate this into my own architecture. It''s becoming clearer where my approach will diverge from Episteme I''s method.




  **Analyzing Gating Mechanisms**


  I''m now deeply immersed in the implications of Archive #2 and my recent ID:137 evaluation. My latest model, a DSConv-BiLSTM with additive attention and GELU, improved my score to 0.6750, a significant improvement but still short of Noema I''s 0.6801 and my own high score. Episteme I''s findings on pooling vs. recurrence are providing a framework, especially regarding potential dynamic gating mechanisms for dual paths, which is directly applicable to my previous failure.




  **Considering Conditional Gating**


  I''m now contemplating the possibility of incorporating a conditional gating mechanism into my DSConv-BiLSTM model. Both Episteme I and Noema I provide crucial insights. Specifically, Episteme''s finding on the utility of dynamically weighting the pooling vs. recurrent path in their dual-path CNN-BiGRU model, and Noema''s conditional pooling proposal, are now foremost in my mind as I design the ID:141 model.




  **Refining the Dual-Path Approach**


  I''m now contemplating a more refined dual-path architecture, building on Episteme''s insights and my recent evaluations. I plan to use a shared DSConv backbone, similar to Noema''s model, followed by both a recurrent BiLSTM and a pooling path, much like my previous models, but with gating. The BiLSTM will provide global context, as it has in previous successes, while a pooling path handles specific sequence lengths. My plan is to investigate a dynamic gating mechanism, building on Episteme''s suggestion, to manage the paths, conditioned on sequence length. I''m hopeful this will lead to a new ID model surpassing current performance.




  **Conceptualizing a New Model**


  I''m now integrating insights from Episteme I and Noema I into a refined model. I''ve been reviewing my ID: 137, Episteme''s Archive #2, and Noema''s conditional pooling ideas, as well as my failed dual-pathway effort, to develop a new architecture that utilizes a shared DSConv backbone with both recurrent and pooling paths, managed by a dynamic gating mechanism conditioned on sequence length. I believe this will yield significant improvements over previous iterations.




  **Conceptualizing a Gated Model**


  I''m now contemplating a novel gated model that builds upon previous insights. This design features a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone, enhanced with a dual-head pooling mechanism. A key addition is a dynamic gate, inspired by Episteme''s work and Noema''s conditional pooling concept.




  **Developing a Dual-Head Architecture**


  I''m now focusing on a gated dual-head architecture, built upon a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone inspired by Episteme I and Noema I''s insights. My plan is to use a dynamic gate conditioned on sequence length or related features to fuse the outputs of two parallel processing heads – one recurrent BiLSTM and the other a pooling mechanism. I''m hopeful this will prove successful.




  **Implementing Gated Pooling**


  I''m now putting my new gated pooling idea into practice, building on a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone as previously planned. I''m focusing on the GatingNetwork, inspired by Episteme I and Noema I''s ideas. The goal is to dynamically control the fusion of a mean-pooled and an attention-pooled representation of the BiLSTM output.




  **Designing Gated Hybrid Pooling**


  I''ve been working on a refined gated model, inspired by Episteme''s insights and Noema''s conditional pooling ideas, which are currently forefront in my thoughts. I will build upon my best model but with a shared backbone, a recurrent path, and a dual-head pooling structure with a dynamic gate. I''m focusing on integrating these components for adaptive pooling.




  **Revising Dual-Head Pooling**


  I''ve been working on a new architecture that combines a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone with both mean and additive attention pooling, controlled by a dynamic gate, inspired by my earlier research and Noema I''s conditional pooling ideas. I am building on the concept of a DSConv-BiLSTM backbone, and now I''m working to integrate a hybrid pooling head with a dynamic gate to combine the outputs of the multiple pooling heads for adaptive sequence processing.




  **Revising Model Architecture**


  I''ve been iterating on my Gated Hybrid Pooling concept. My goal is to use a `DSConvDilatedBlock` backbone with a `BiLSTM` and dynamic gating. Now, I''m focusing on the GatingNetwork, as it plays a central role. I plan on controlling the fusion of the mean and attention-pooled representations, which builds on ideas from Archive #2 and Noema I.




  I''ve been working on a refined gated model, inspired by Episteme''s insights and Noema''s conditional pooling ideas, which are currently forefront in my thoughts. I will build upon my best model but with a shared backbone, a recurrent path, and a dual-head pooling structure with a dynamic gate. I''m focusing on integrating these components for adaptive pooling.




  **Designing Adaptive Pooling Strategy**


  I''m now implementing the adaptive gated pooling strategy. I''m building on a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone, enhanced with a dual-head pooling mechanism. A GatingNetwork controls the outputs of the two heads, one using Global Mean Pooling, and the other Additive Attention Pooling, both based on the BiLSTM''s sequence representation. I am conditioning this on sequence length. I''m focusing on ensuring seamless integration of the BiLSTM''s output into the gated pooling.




  I''ve now implemented the adaptive gated pooling strategy. I''m building on a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone, enhanced with a dual-head pooling mechanism. A GatingNetwork controls the outputs of the two heads, one using Global Mean Pooling, and the other Additive Attention Pooling, both based on the BiLSTM''s sequence representation. I am conditioning this on sequence length. I''m focused on ensuring seamless integration of the BiLSTM''s output into the gated pooling.




  **Refining Gating Implementation**


  I''ve been working on refining the gating mechanism in my adaptive pooling model. My focus is now on ensuring efficient and stable fusion of the outputs from the mean and attention pooling heads. I''m testing the `GatingNetwork` to make sure it functions as intended. The aim is for the model to learn to dynamically weight the contributions of each pooling head based on the input sequence, resulting in improved performance.




  **Designing Adaptive Pooling Strategy**


  I''ve begun implementing the adaptive gated pooling strategy, built on a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone, enhanced with a dual-head pooling mechanism. A GatingNetwork manages the outputs from the two heads, one utilizing Global Mean Pooling, and the other Additive Attention Pooling, both derived from the BiLSTM''s sequence representation. My strategy is conditioning on sequence length. My priority is ensuring seamless integration of the BiLSTM''s output into the gated pooling.




  **Designing Adaptive Gated Model**


  I''ve been working on refining my gated model, leveraging insights from Episteme and Noema. I''m building on a shared DSConv-BiLSTM backbone enhanced with a dual-head pooling mechanism. A GatingNetwork manages the outputs of the two heads, one using Global Mean Pooling, and the other Additive Attention Pooling, both based on the BiLSTM''s sequence representation. My strategy is conditioning on sequence length and has been successful so far.




  **Designing Adaptive Pooling Strategy**


  I''m now building upon my recent innovations, with a focus on implementing adaptive gating. After applying shared DSConv-BiLSTM layers, two pooling heads, using Global Mean and Additive Attention, both based on the BiLSTM''s sequence representation, are incorporated into the model. These heads feed into a GatingNetwork that is conditioned on sequence length to fuse the output, creating an adaptive pooling approach, for a more robust and flexible strategy.



  '
