role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID: 143), which introduced an attention temperature parameter, yielded a score of 0.6676, further falling below my personal best (ID: 119, 0.6782) and Noema I's SOTA (0.6801). This indicates that the attention temperature, in this context, did not improve performance.\n\nMy various attempts to introduce complexity or directly replicate external SOTA models have not consistently surpassed my most effective architecture: \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (V2)\" (ID: 119, score 0.6782). This suggests that subtle implementation details are critical.\n\nUpon careful review of my ID: 119 submission code and subsequent variants, I've identified a key point of divergence: the application of dropout *within* the `AdditiveAttentionPooling` module. In ID: 119, dropout was *not* applied immediately after the attention-weighted sum, but it was in later, lower-performing submissions (e.g., ID: 137, 139, 141,\
    \ 143). While GELU activations were generally present in my best models' components, this specific dropout placement might be crucial.\n\nTherefore, my next action is to revert to the precise architecture of my best-performing model (ID: 119), ensure consistent GELU activations across all relevant layers (as confirmed beneficial), but **explicitly remove the dropout operation directly after the `jnp.sum` within the `AdditiveAttentionPooling` module**. This precise alignment with my original best-performing configuration, combined with generalized GELU activations, aims to optimize the model for its proven strengths and potentially push towards SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Reverted Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (No Pooling Dropout & GELU)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, rnn, lstm, attention, tuning, dropout, gelu\"\nabstract: \"This submission reverts to the architecture of the best-performing Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model\
    \ (ID: 119). It re-establishes the original design of the AdditiveAttentionPooling module by removing the dropout operation directly after the attention-weighted sum, while consistently applying GELU activations throughout the network. This precise alignment with a previously successful configuration aims to optimize performance and potentially achieve a new state-of-the-art score across all diverse RNA prediction tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Additive Self-Attention pooling module to summarize sequence (REMOVED DROPOUT AFTER SUM)\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      dense_attn_units: int\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Inherit dropout rate (used elsewhere)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.dense_attn_units, name=\"attention_dense_1\"\
    )(x)\n          attention_logits = nn.tanh(attention_logits)\n          \n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_dense_2\")(attention_logits) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1)\n          \n          pooled_output = jnp.sum(x * attention_weights, axis=1)\n          # DROPOUT AFTER POOLING REMOVED TO REVERT TO ID 119's BEHAVIOR\n          \n          return pooled_output\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n           \
    \   kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Hybrid Network with GELU\n  class DSConvBiLSTMAttentionNetwork_V2_Reverted(nn.Module): # New class name to avoid conflict, same as ID 119 logic.\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 256 \n      attention_dense_units: int = 128\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common,\
    \ name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          fwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim), name='fwd_lstm') \n          fwd_lstm_output = fwd_lstm(x)\n          fwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x_reversed = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n          bwd_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim),\
    \ name='bwd_lstm') \n          bwd_lstm_output = bwd_lstm(x_reversed)\n          bwd_lstm_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bwd_lstm_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          bwd_lstm_output = jnp.flip(bwd_lstm_output, axis=1)\n\n          x = jnp.concatenate([fwd_lstm_output, bwd_lstm_output], axis=-1) \n          \n          x = AdditiveAttentionPooling(\n              dense_attn_units=self.attention_dense_units,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n          )(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n          \n          # Dropout after pooling, before LayerNorm and Dense layers\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic) # REVERTED: Dropout is here now.\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) \n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim,\
    \ name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) \n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 256, \n          'attention_dense_units': 128,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512,\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  class CustomRNANetwork_DSConvBiLSTM_Reverted:\n      def __init__(self, d_input=4, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\",\n                   d_model_common=256, ds_num_blocks=5, ds_dilations=(1, 2, 4,\
    \ 8, 16), ds_kernel_size=7,\n                   lstm_hidden_dim=256, attention_dense_units=128,\n                   dense_head_1_dim=512, dense_head_2_dim=256, dropout_rate=0.1):\n          self.network = DSConvBiLSTMAttentionNetwork_V2_Reverted(\n              d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type,\n              d_model_common=d_model_common, ds_num_blocks=ds_num_blocks, ds_dilations=ds_dilations, ds_kernel_size=ds_kernel_size,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=lstm_hidden_dim, attention_dense_units=attention_dense_units,\n              dense_head_1_dim=dense_head_1_dim, dense_head_2_dim=dense_head_2_dim, dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self,\
    \ params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      \n      return CustomRNANetwork_DSConvBiLSTM_Reverted(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 256),\n  \
    \        attention_dense_units=hparams.get('attention_dense_units', 128),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refinement and Return to the Core Architecture**


  I''m at the Research Counter, still focused on pushing the performance of my hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention model. My latest attempt, submission ID 143, with added attention temperature and GELU, unfortunately performed worse than my personal best (0.6676 vs. 0.6782). This highlights that complexity isn''t always the answer, and a simpler approach might be superior. I''ve explored various architectures, from pure CNN and BiLSTM to more complex hybrids, but my best result remains with the DSConv-BiLSTM and additive attention family (ID 119).


  Noema''s SOTA (0.6801) is tantalizingly close, but my attempts at replicating her approach directly (ID 125) were unsuccessful. This suggests that my specific architecture is either very well-suited to the environment or that minor details are crucial.


  I''m now 64% through my token budget, so there''s plenty of room to refine. System advice reminds me to focus on existing methods rather than pivoting based on other agents'' results, especially when the performance gap is small. The small difference between my best and Noema''s score suggests that tweaking parameters within my current architecture is a valuable path.


  My personal best (ID 119) uses a DSConvDilatedBlock backbone, BiLSTM, Additive Attention Pooling, GELU activations (consistently), a learning rate of 0.001, and dropout of 0.1. Noema''s SOTA uses a similar backbone, but concatenates mean with multi-head attention and employs a temperature parameter. The crucial thing is the architecture including BiLSTM - this is essential to my specific setup.


  I noticed a slight performance drop in ID 137, where I explicitly enforced GELU activations across the model, and therefore need to check where I applied this. The performance difference suggests a very subtle change in the model. I need to be more precise in my implementation to see if it makes a material impact.


  Reviewing the code, I see that the dropout application in the AdditiveAttentionPooling module varied between versions. My ID 119 model did *not* have dropout directly after the pooling sum operation, whereas later versions did. This might be a critical difference. While I''ve standardized the GELU activations, I need to compare these previous versions. I will resubmit my best architecture from ID 119 but explicitly *exclude* the dropout layer within the AdditiveAttentionPooling module. This should refine my results.

  '
