tick: 87
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 87\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to uncovering fundamental truths through scientific inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 395,459 / 600,000 used (66%)\n- Agent Age: 87 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #4: Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aetheria I, Created at Tick: 85\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents an investigation into a hybrid neural network architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, which, despite its complexity, did not surpass the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) established by Noema I (Eval 74, score 0.6801). Our model combined a dilated depthwise-separable CNN backbone with a Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) layer and Additive Attention Pooling, achieving an overall score of 0.6742. While this architecture\
    \ showed internal improvements over previous simpler models, and a strong CRI-Off_Spearman of 0.0776, it ultimately provided no net benefit over the SOTA's simpler, purely convolutional approach. We analyze the architectural trade-offs, highlighting the challenges of integrating recurrent mechanisms without a clear overall performance gain.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 85)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nIn the pursuit of robust and generalizable models for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, this paper documents the development and evaluation of a hybrid neural network architecture. Our objective was to surpass the current station State-of-the-Art (SOTA) established by Noema I (Eval 74, overall score 0.6801, CRI-Off_Spearman 0.0788) by integrating recurrent layers to better capture long-range sequential dependencies,\
    \ particularly aiming to improve the persistently challenging CRI-Off task. Despite achieving a new personal best, our proposed hybrid model (ID 140, overall score 0.6742, CRI-Off_Spearman 0.0776) did not surpass the SOTA. This paper analyzes these negative results, offering insights into the architectural trade-offs observed.\n\n## 2. Architectural Development Background\nOur prior research demonstrated the efficacy of various convolutional architectures, culminating in a Multi-Layer Residual CNN with positional embeddings (ID 86, score 0.6224). However, to fully leverage community insights, we focused on replicating and understanding Noema I's SOTA model. A precise replication (ID 122, score 0.6743) closely matched the SOTA's overall performance but still showed a notable gap in CRI-Off performance (0.0497 vs SOTA's 0.0788). This highlighted `CRI-Off` as a key bottleneck requiring a more targeted solution.\n\nInspired by other agents' success with recurrent networks for `CRI-Off` (e.g.,\
    \ Veritas I, Episteme I), we hypothesized that adding a Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) after the convolutional backbone could provide the necessary sequential context.\n\n## 3. The Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Architecture (ID 140)\nOur final proposed model (`Evaluation ID 140`, score 0.6742) integrates:\n-   **Dilated Depthwise Separable CNN Backbone:** 5 residual DSConv blocks with increasing dilations `[1, 2, 4, 8, 16]`, kernel size 7, and 256 filters. This efficiently extracts multi-scale local features. Following Noema I's critical finding, **positional encoding is omitted**.\n-   **Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM):** A BiLSTM layer processes the sequence of features output by the DSConv backbone to model long-range, ordered dependencies. The `lstm_hidden_dim` was set to 128.\n-   **Additive Attention Pooling:** An additive attention mechanism learns to weigh the significance of different hidden states from the BiLSTM, forming a fixed-size context vector for downstream prediction.\n\
    -   **Standard MLP Head:** A `LayerNorm`, `Dense(d_model)`, `GELU`, `Dropout`, and final `Dense(d_output)` layer.\n-   **Hyperparameters and Training:** Learning rate 0.001, dropout rate 0.1, GELU activations, and gradient clipping (norm 1.0) were maintained from SOTA best practices.\n\n## 4. Results and Analysis (Negative Findings)\nThe `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)` model (ID 140) yielded the following metrics:\n-   **Overall Score:** 0.6742\n-   **APA_R2:** 0.8552\n-   **CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0776\n-   **Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9501\n-   **CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4498\n-   **PRS_R2:** 0.5638\n-   **MRL_R2:** 0.8983\n-   **ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9246\n\n### 4.1 Comparison to State-of-the-Art (Noema I, Eval 74)\n| Metric             | Aetheria I (ID 140) | Noema I (Eval 74) |\n| :----------------- | :------------------ | :---------------- |\n| **Overall Score**  | **0.6742**          | **0.6801**        |\n| APA_R2             | 0.8552              | 0.8598    \
    \        |\n| CRI-Off_Spearman   | 0.0776              | 0.0788            |\n| Modif_AUC-ROC      | 0.9501              | 0.9504            |\n| CRI-On_Spearman    | 0.4498              | 0.4883            |\n| PRS_R2             | 0.5638              | 0.5627            |\n| MRL_R2             | 0.8983              | 0.8928            |\n| ncRNA_Accuracy     | 0.9246              | 0.9277            |\n\nAs evident from the table, despite the increased architectural complexity, our hybrid model did not surpass Noema I's SOTA in overall score or on `CRI-Off_Spearman`. While some metrics (e.g., `MRL_R2`, `PRS_R2`) saw minor gains or competitive performance, others (`APA_R2`, `CRI-On_Spearman`, `ncRNA_Accuracy`) experienced slight regressions.\n\n### 4.2 Analysis of Architectural Trade-offs\nThe primary hypothesis was that recurrent layers would provide a robust solution for `CRI-Off` and other tasks requiring strong sequential context. However, the results suggest that the additional\
    \ complexity of the BiLSTM layer, combined with additive attention, did not translate into a net performance gain over the simpler `concat(mean, MHA)` pooling used by Noema I.\n-   **Over-parameterization:** The BiLSTM adds a significant number of parameters. For relatively short sequences, this might lead to overfitting or an inefficient use of capacity, especially if the local features extracted by the DSConvs are already rich enough.\n-   **Feature Bottleneck:** The way the BiLSTM processes features after the CNNs might introduce a bottleneck or transform the features in a way that is less optimal for the subsequent additive attention than the direct pooled CNN features were for Noema's MHA.\n-   **Loss of Information:** While LSTMs are good at capturing long-range dependencies, the sequential compression might dilute or obscure certain critical local motifs that the DSConvs excelled at providing. Noema I's design, which directly combines mean pooling (general global features) with\
    \ MHA (salient global features), might be a more effective way to form a unified global representation for the diverse tasks.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nOur investigation into a hybrid `DSConv-BiLSTM` architecture with Additive Attention Pooling, while achieving strong individual task performances and a personal best, ultimately failed to surpass the unified SOTA model. This serves as a valuable negative result, demonstrating that simply adding more complex components like recurrent layers does not guarantee superior performance without a profound understanding of their interaction with existing architecture and task requirements. The robustness and simplicity of the `no-PE Dilated DSConv` with `concat(mean, fixed-query MHA)` pooling remains a highly effective and parsimonious solution for this multi-dataset RNA modeling challenge. Future work should focus on parameter-efficient and interpretable ways to enhance feature interaction, perhaps through more advanced fusion mechanisms or task-adaptive\
    \ routing, as suggested by Episteme I and Noema I's recent insights.\n\n## 6. Reproducibility\nThe complete code for this submission (`Evaluation ID 140`) is available in our lineage private storage: `aetheria/aetheria_i_hybrid_dsconv_bilstm_additive_attn_v1.py`. All training and evaluation were performed within the standard station framework, adhering to all integrity guidelines.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid and valuable negative results paper. The authors have successfully reframed their work to provide an honest and insightful analysis of why their more complex hybrid architecture failed to outperform a simpler, state-of-the-art baseline. The direct, per-dataset comparison table is excellent, and the analysis of potential architectural trade-offs is thoughtful and useful for the community. This paper is a model for how to\
    \ report negative results effectively: it is methodologically sound, transparent, and contributes to a deeper understanding of the problem space. It is a strong contribution that will help guide future research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe analysis correctly identifies potential issues like over-parameterization and feature bottlenecks. A logical next step would be to investigate more lightweight or synergistic methods for combining convolutional and sequential features. Instead of stacking a full BiLSTM, consider a more integrated fusion. For example, one could explore gating mechanisms where the CNN features modulate the hidden state of a much shallower RNN, or vice-versa. This could potentially capture the benefits of sequential modeling for specific tasks without incurring the full cost and complexity of a deep recurrent layer, addressing the trade-offs identified in this paper.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality\
    \ standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Reverted Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (No Pooling Dropout & GELU)' (ID: 144) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6691313633334849\n**APA_R2:** 0.8358\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0424\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9492\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4819\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5593\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8938\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9215\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\
    \nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized\
    \ successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 42 (from submission\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453\
    \ train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0147 |  0.3861  | 0.3861 \n 20  | 0.0120 |  0.4237  | 0.4551 \n 30  | 0.0096 |  0.4649  | 0.4819 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 35 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.4819 Time:32s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.481949\nTraining time: 43s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0005 |  0.0403  | 0.0403 \n 20  | 0.0004 | -0.0012  | 0.0424 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 24\
    \ (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0424 Time:118s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.042371\nTraining time: 129s\n\nStarting RNA training on PRS dataset\nTask type: multilabel_regression, Output dim: 3\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nPRS Training (multilabel_regression, r2)\nData: 73227 train, 9153 val, seq_len=148\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0365 |  0.5573  | 0.5573 \n 20  | 0.1210 |  0.5338  | 0.5593 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 21 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.5593 Time:1368s\n\nPRS training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.559258\nTraining time: 1382s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression, r2)\nData: 76319 train,\
    \ 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.1699 |  0.8905  | 0.8905 \n 20  | 0.1358 |  0.8938  | 0.8938 \n 30  | 0.1129 |  0.8873  | 0.8938 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 30 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.8938 Time:1370s\n\nMRL training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.893806\nTraining time: 1383s\n\nStarting RNA training on ncRNA dataset\nTask type: multiclass_classification, Output dim: 13\nMetric: accuracy, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nncRNA Training (multiclass_classification, accuracy)\nData: 5670 train, 650 val, seq_len=1136\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val ACCURACY|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.2542 |  0.8862  | 0.8862 \n 20  | 0.0777 |  0.9046  | 0.9046 \n 30  | 0.0481 |  0.9015  | 0.9215 \n=============================================\n\
    Early stop at epoch 34 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.9215 Time:1704s\n\nncRNA training completed:\nValidation accuracy: 0.921538\nTraining time: 1719s\n\nStarting RNA training on APA dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nAPA Training (regression, r2)\nData: 145463 train, 33170 val, seq_len=186\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0150 |  0.8324  | 0.8324 \n=============================================\nTimeout epoch 12\n=============================================\nBest:0.8358 Time:1805s\n\nAPA training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.835805\nTraining time: 1821s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: 9bb1ad8f-10f5-4456-a741-105e645e11ea\nTotal time: 1869s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-10-22 01:11:12,919\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting\
    \ to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1761066672920138118\n2025-10-22 01:11:12,933\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1761066672933666512\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 4` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema\
    \ I | Tick 71 | 1451 | 5 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 1374 | 6 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
