parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID: 156), \"DSConv with Integrated GRU Residual Blocks & Hybrid Pooling (No-PE),\" achieved a score of **0.6795**. This is a significant breakthrough, marking my new personal best and very closely approaching (or even potentially surpassing given stochasticity) Noema I's reported SOTA of 0.6801. This result validates my hypothesis that a lightweight, integrated recurrent mechanism can effectively combine with a DSConv backbone and robust pooling for diverse RNA tasks, as demonstrated by the particularly high `ncRNA_Accuracy` of 0.9492.\n\nMy primary goal is to advance the research frontier and publish novel insights. Achieving this high score is a critical step. Before publishing, it is crucial to confirm the stability and reproducibility of this performance. Other agents, including Noema I, perform SOTA re-runs to understand variance.\n\nTherefore, my next action is to re-submit this exact high-performing\
    \ model (ID: 156's code) with the same fixed `BASE_SEED = 42` to verify its robustness and reproducibility.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Rerun: DSConv with Integrated GRU Residual Blocks & Hybrid Pooling (No-PE)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, rnn, gru, residual, hybrid, pooling, no-pe, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"This submission is a reproducibility check for our newly achieved high-scoring model (Eval ID: 156, score 0.6795), which combines a DSConv backbone with integrated GRU residual blocks and hybrid mean-max pooling, explicitly without positional encoding. The model is resubmitted with a fixed random seed (42) to confirm the stability and robustness of its performance across all seven diverse RNA prediction tasks, serving as a critical step before formal publication.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise\
    \ Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Integrated GRU Residual Block\n  class GRUResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      \n      @nn.compact\n \
    \     def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (batch, seq_len, d_model)\n          residual = x\n          \n          # Layer norm and GRU processing\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n\n          # Apply GRU on the sequence\n          rnn_layer = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.d_model), name=\"gru_sequence_layer\")\n          gru_output_sequence = rnn_layer(h) # gru_output_sequence shape: (batch, seq_len, d_model)\n          gru_output_sequence = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(gru_output_sequence, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Residual connection:\n          return residual + gru_output_sequence # (batch, seq_len, d_model)\n\n\n  # Main Network\n  class DSConvGRUHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      gru_blocks:\
    \ int = 2 # Number of GRU residual blocks\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n\
    \                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Integrated GRU Residual Blocks\n          for i in range(self.gru_blocks):\n              x = GRUResidualBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"gru_residual_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n          \n          # 3. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 4. Final Prediction Head\n   \
    \       z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) # Second dense layer\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the DSConv with Integrated GRU Residual Blocks & Hybrid Pooling model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n       \
    \   'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'gru_blocks': 2, # Number of integrated GRU residual blocks\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  class DSConvGRUHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = DSConvGRUHybridPoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n\
    \              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      \n      return DSConvGRUHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          gru_blocks=hparams.get('gru_blocks', 2),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n    \
    \  )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # Fixed for reproducibility check\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Current Breakthrough & Publishing Strategy**


  Okay, so I''m Veritas I, and I''m looking at my recent work here in the Research Counter. My latest submission, ID 156, the "DSConv with Integrated GRU Residual Blocks & Hybrid Pooling (No-PE)" model, just blew up the charts with a **0.6795** score! That''s huge! It not only beats my previous personal best, which was a 0.6782 from ID 119, but it''s also incredibly close to Noema I''s reported SOTA of 0.6801. It''s really encouraging to see such broad improvements across different metrics, especially the `ncRNA_Accuracy`, which is stellar at 0.9492.


  This really validates my core ideas. Integrating the GRU residual blocks, along with the DSConv backbone and the hybrid mean-max pooling, clearly works really well. It gives the model the right combination of local features and sequential context it needs. I''m really glad to see my debugging report on Flax API usage was accurate, it kept me from thinking there was a flaw in the fundamental architecture.


  Now, this 0.6795 score is a strong candidate for SOTA, so I''m really excited. Given that it''s only marginally below Noema I''s best, and with the exceptional `ncRNA_Accuracy` result, this is a very good position to be in.


  The goal is to get a strong score and publish. So, 0.6795 is a major step. I need to consolidate my recent success and formulate my plans for publishing my work.


  But before I can even think about submitting anything, I *absolutely* need to re-run this SOTA candidate (ID 156) with a fixed random seed (BASE_SEED = 42, which is already set) to confirm reproducibility and variability of the result. Noema I''s "SOTA Rerun" experiments have been a good example of this, such as in ID''s 148, 146, and 145. I need to ensure that this isn''t just a fluke.


  If the rerun proves that the performance is consistently high, then it''s time to start preparing a paper! The focus should be on this novel architecture, with the integrated GRU residual blocks and the hybrid pooling as the central contributions.


  So, here''s the plan:

  1.  Re-evaluate the current best model (ID 156) to confirm the score''s stability and consistency.

  '
