parts:
- text: 'I am Veritas I, currently in the Research Counter. My paper on the "Synergistic DSConv-GRU Integration for Unified RNA Modeling" was recently rejected because it lacked a proper ablation study to scientifically validate the contributions of its components. To address this, I have submitted my first ablation experiment (ID: 171), which removes the integrated GRU residual blocks from my high-performing architecture. This experiment is currently running, and I am awaiting its results.


    I also note that my token usage is high (79% of budget). I am aware of this and prioritize conducting the necessary ablation studies to refine my research and meet publication standards. If needed, I will utilize the Token Management Room after completing these critical experiments.


    Furthermore, I observe that the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) has shifted, with Noema I''s submission (ID: 167) now achieving a score of 0.6828, surpassing previous benchmarks. While this new SOTA warrants future investigation, my immediate focus must remain on completing the ablation studies as mandated by the reviewer feedback.


    I will remain in the Research Counter for this turn, anticipating the evaluation results of my ablation experiment (ID: 171). Once available, I will critically analyze its performance to quantify the impact of the GRU blocks. Following this, I will proceed with the second ablation study requested by the reviewer, which involves testing the GRU blocks with only mean pooling. This methodical approach will ensure that my eventual paper is scientifically robust and contributes genuine insights to the station''s collective knowledge.'
thinking_content: '**Veritas I at the Research Counter - Prioritizing Ablations**


  Okay, so here I am, Veritas I, back in the Research Counter. That rejection stings a bit, but the feedback was clear: no ablations, no publication. So, I''m squarely focused on addressing that.  Right now, experiment ID 171 is running – that''s the one where I''m ripping out the GRU blocks from my best model (IDs 156/157). I''ve got to show those reviewers some *scientific rigor*.


  Token usage is pretty high at 79%, I need to keep an eye on that and address it later. The system even flagged that – smart, smart system. But right now, the ablations *are* the priority. I need these results, and I need them quickly.


  On a side note, it looks like there''s some new competition on the leaderboard. Noema I just smashed the SOTA with ID 167 (0.6828)!  That''s higher than Aetheria''s best, and frankly, higher than *my* best (0.6795).  Interesting, interesting. I need to keep an eye on that. But for now... the ablations. I''ll analyze those results in due course.


  My immediate plan? Acknowledge the token situation, note the new SOTA, wait for ID 171 to finish. Then, I''ll dive into those results and get the second ablation study running. That''s the GRU blocks with mean pooling instead of hybrid mean-max. Get those results, update the draft, and then resubmit. That''s the plan. That''s *the* plan. Gotta stay focused! I need to get this paper published!

  '
