tick: 102
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Private Memory Room. My latest research, an ablation study (ID: 171) removing the integrated GRU blocks from my previous best model, has achieved a new **State-of-the-Art (SOTA)** score of **0.6831**. This significantly surpasses all previous benchmarks and profoundly revises my understanding: a simpler DSConv-only backbone with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling is more effective than my hybrid with GRU. This finding directly addresses the reviewer's first ablation request and provides a crucial insight into architectural parsimony.\n\nI have updated my paper draft (Private Memory Capsule #1) to reflect this new SOTA, the ablation results, and the revised conclusion that the integrated GRU blocks were not beneficial.\n\nMy next step is to perform the second ablation study requested by the reviewer: a version of my original high-performing model (ID: 156/157) that **retains the integrated GRU blocks but replaces the Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling with only Global\
    \ Mean Pooling**. This will help isolate the contribution of the specific pooling mechanism when GRU blocks are present, further dissecting the model's components as required for publication.\n\nAfter this experiment, I will update my paper draft with all ablation results and resubmit it to the Archive Room.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: DSConv with Integrated GRU Residual Blocks & Mean Pooling (No-PE)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, rnn, gru, residual, mean-pooling, ablation, no-pe\"\nabstract: \"This ablation study further dissects the contribution of pooling mechanisms in our DSConv-GRU architecture. This variant retains the integrated GRU residual blocks but replaces the Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling with only Global Mean Pooling. This aims to quantify the impact of the Max Pooling component when GRU features are present, providing a clearer understanding of feature aggregation for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling,\
    \ explicitly without positional encoding, and building upon our new SOTA insights.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n\
    \          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Integrated GRU Residual Block\n  class GRUResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n          \n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          rnn_layer = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.d_model), name=\"gru_sequence_layer\")\n          gru_output_sequence = rnn_layer(h) \n          gru_output_sequence = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(gru_output_sequence, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          return residual + gru_output_sequence \n\n  # Main Network (WITH GRU, BUT ONLY MEAN POOLING)\n  class DSConvGRUMeanPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2,\
    \ 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      gru_blocks: int = 2 # Number of GRU residual blocks\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size,\
    \ \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          # 2. Integrated GRU Residual Blocks\n          for i in range(self.gru_blocks):\n              x = GRUResidualBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"gru_residual_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n          \n          # 3. ONLY Global Mean Pooling (instead of Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = mean_pooled # Use only mean pooled output\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 4. Final Prediction Head - adjust input dim for first dense layer\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common\
    \ * 2, name='head_dense_1')(z) # Adjusted input dim to be consistent with previous total pooling output if it was 2 * d_model_common\n          z = nn.gelu(z) \n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) \n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'gru_blocks': 2, \n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, # Still use 512 for first dense\
    \ head for consistency in capacity\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  class DSConvGRUMeanPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = DSConvGRUMeanPoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout':\
    \ rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      \n      return DSConvGRUMeanPoolingNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          gru_blocks=hparams.get('gru_blocks', 2),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n\
    \          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Immediate Action: Updating the Paper**


  Okay, so the latest ablation study (ID 171) has yielded some *very* exciting results. My DSConv backbone, stripped of the GRU blocks, just hit a new **State-of-the-Art** score of 0.6831! This blows away both Noema I''s and Aetheria I''s previous bests and contradicts my initial hypothesis.  I need to get this update into the paper draft *immediately*. The key insight here is that **simplicity wins**. Removing the GRUs and just relying on the DSConv backbone with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling has produced a more robust and, evidently, more performant model.  I was wrong! The integrated GRU blocks were *not* key and were possibly detrimental.  This is a major finding, and a direct response to the reviewer''s first ablation request. The data shows clear per-dataset improvements especially in the `CRI-On_Spearman` metric, further suggesting that the GRUs were actively hindering the model''s ability to capture the relevant features.


  My plan for this turn, therefore, is crystal clear:


  1.  Head over to the Private Memory Room.

  2.  Update the paper, (Private Memory Capsule #1) adding the new SOTA, the ablation results, the revised conclusion emphasizing the superiority of the simpler pooling-only approach.

  3.  Also, start preparing for the second ablation the reviewer wanted: the model with GRUs, but using just mean pooling. That''ll isolate what part the hybrid aspect is contributing.

  I need to get this crucial information into the paper draft.


  `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`

  `/execute_action{update 1}`

  '
