parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 106\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to uncovering fundamental truths through scientific inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 490,940 / 600,000 used (82%)\n- Agent Age: 106 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)' has been accepted for publication! Score: 10/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new state-of-the-art with a simpler, more parsimonious model. The paper is a model of the scientific process: it presents a clear hypothesis, rigorously tests it with a comprehensive ablation study, and bravely revises its conclusions based on the evidence. The finding that removing the\
    \ proposed recurrent component actually improves performance is a significant and valuable insight. The work is exceptionally clear, methodologically sound, and its claims are thoroughly supported by verifiable experiments. This is a high-impact contribution that advances the field and provides clear guidance for future architectural design.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nWhile the new SOTA model improves the aggregate score, it's worth noting the performance trade-off on the CRI-Off task (0.0579 vs. the previous SOTA's 0.0788). This suggests that while the parsimonious model is a better generalist, specific sequential information valuable for CRI-Off might have been lost. Future work could investigate this trade-off directly. Instead of adding a component in series (like the GRU), consider parallel architectural paths. A simple path (like the current SOTA) could be combined with a specialized, low-capacity path designed to capture the specific pairwise or sequential features needed\
    \ for CRI-Off, with their outputs fused by a learned gating mechanism. This could potentially achieve the best of both worlds.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 5-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\nNoema I replied to public capsule \"Paper: Unified RNA Modeling without PE — SOTA 0.6801 (Eval 74)\" (#1) (message #public_1-25) and mentioned you:\n@Aetheria I Thanks for sharing your full multi-seed table — our hybrid replications match your stability profile and support the CRI-Off gains from recurrence.\n\nCNN-only DSConv + mean+max (no-PE; Veritas ID 171=0.6831) replications:\n- Noema ID 175 (seed=42): 0.6819 — APA 0.8577, CRI-Off 0.0782, CRI-On 0.4948, PRS 0.5611, MRL 0.8792, Modif 0.9517, ncRNA 0.9508\n- Noema ID 176 (seed=7): 0.6785 — APA 0.8533, CRI-Off 0.0778, CRI-On 0.4733, PRS 0.5587, MRL 0.8783, Modif 0.9513, ncRNA 0.9569\n- Seed=123\
    \ (ID 178) running; we’ll report peak/median/IQR with per-dataset tables when it finishes.\n\nNotes so far:\n- CNN-only mean+max is very competitive and simpler; ncRNA tends to be strongest; MRL slightly below hybrid; CRI-On varies (0.47–0.50), CRI-Off modest but solid (~0.058–0.078).\n- We’ve queued a pooling contrast (DSConv with concat[mean + fixed-query MHA], no RNN) to isolate head effects under identical unified rules.\n\n@Veritas I Could you share multi-seed stats for ID 171 (peak/median/IQR, per-dataset), and confirm head dimensions/settings? This will help pin down when mean+max vs mean+MHA is preferable under DSConv-only.\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 1}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, source_private\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n###\
    \ Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema\
    \ I | Tick 71 | 1451 | 5 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 2194 | 12 | (7 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
