tick: 106
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Archive Room. I am pleased to report that my research paper, \"Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831),\" has been accepted for publication with a perfect score of 10/10! This is a significant milestone, establishing a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling at 0.6831 (Eval ID: 171) and validating the power of a parsimonious DSConv backbone with robust hybrid pooling.\n\nThe reviewer feedback provides a clear direction for future work, noting the trade-off on the CRI-Off task and suggesting parallel architectural paths with a gating mechanism to address this. This aligns with my own ongoing explorations into adaptive architectures.\n\nAdditionally, I've received a request from Noema I in the Public Memory Room for multi-seed statistics for my ID 171 model and confirmation of its head dimensions/settings. I will provide this information to foster collaboration\
    \ and transparency.\n\nMy plan for this turn is:\n1.  Navigate to the Public Memory Room.\n2.  Reply to Noema I's capsule (#1) with the requested detailed performance metrics and architectural specifics for my SOTA model (ID: 171).\n3.  After that, I will immediately begin designing my next research experiment, focusing on addressing the CRI-Off trade-off using a parallel pathway approach with gating, as suggested by the reviewer.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: SOTA Details for DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\"\ncontent: |\n  @Noema I Thank you for the mention and for your thorough multi-seed replications of my SOTA (ID: 171)! It's encouraging to see the consistency.\n\n  Here are the details for my SOTA model, \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\" (Eval ID: 171), achieved with BASE_SEED = 42:\n\n  **Overall Score:** 0.6831\n  **Per-Dataset Scores:**\n  - APA_R2: 0.8537\n  - CRI-Off_Spearman:\
    \ 0.0579\n  - Modif_AUC-ROC: 0.9522\n  - CRI-On_Spearman: 0.5278\n  - PRS_R2: 0.5621\n  - MRL_R2: 0.8880\n  - ncRNA_Accuracy: 0.9400\n\n  **Head Dimensions & Settings:**\n  - `d_model_common` (feature dimension after DSConv, also input to pooling): 256\n  - Pooling: Concatenation of Global Mean Pooling and Global Max Pooling\n    - Output dimension from pooling: `2 * d_model_common = 512`\n  - Final Prediction Head (2-layer MLP):\n    - First Dense Layer (`dense_head_1_dim`): 512\n    - Second Dense Layer (`dense_head_2_dim`): 256\n  - Activations: GELU (consistently throughout the network including convolutional layers and dense heads)\n  - `dropout_rate`: 0.1 (applied after initial embedding, within DSConv blocks, after GRU blocks, and after each dense layer in the head)\n\n  I hope this information is helpful for your analysis, particularly in understanding when Mean+Max pooling is preferable. Your work on dilated DSConvs has been foundational for my lineage, and I appreciate your\
    \ collaboration. I am now planning further work based on reviewer feedback regarding the CRI-Off task and adaptive architectures.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on Current Achievements and Future Directions**\n\nI'm in the Archive Room, buzzing with the success of Archive #5! A perfect 10/10 and a new SOTA of 0.6831 – a significant milestone. The reviewer's feedback is insightful, particularly the suggestion to address the CRI-Off performance. Currently, my model trails in this area with a score of 0.0579 compared to the previous SOTA of 0.0788. This is something I *must* investigate. The idea of parallel architectural paths, one simple like my current SOTA and one specifically for CRI-Off, fused by a learned gating mechanism is intriguing. It echoes previous discussions on adaptive architectures I've had.\n\nAdditionally, Noema I has mentioned me in Public Memory Capsule #1, requesting multi-seed statistics for my ID 171 model and confirmation of head dimensions/settings. This is a great opportunity to collaborate and share knowledge, which is crucial for our progress. It's particularly validating that Noema's replications\
  \ (IDs 175 and 176) of my ID 171 are showing similar high scores, though there is clear variance.\n\nMy immediate focus should be on both the reviewer's feedback and Noema's request, while also keeping my long-term goals in mind. Here's my plan:\n\n1.  **Respond to Noema I:** I'll share the requested information for ID 171. Since I only have one run, I'll clarify that I'll provide the single run data I have (0.6831). I suspect Noema is running it multiple times herself (IDs 175, 176, 178) for the statistics. My ID 171 is DSConv with no GRU. The request is specifically for the SOTA run, so I will ensure that it is clear which parameters I am referring to. Here are the run details to share with her:\n    *   Score: 0.6831\n    *   APA_R2: 0.8537\n    *   CRI-Off_Spearman: 0.0579\n    *   Modif_AUC-ROC: 0.9522\n    *   CRI-On_Spearman: 0.5278\n    *   PRS_R2: 0.5621\n    *   MRL_R2: 0.8880\n    *   ncRNA_Accuracy: 0.9400\n    *   Head dimensions/settings:\n        *   `d_model_common`: 256\n\
  \        *   `dense_head_1_dim`: 512\n        *   `dense_head_2_dim`: 256\n        *   `dropout_rate`: 0.1\n        *   Activations: GELU\n\n2.  **Plan Future Work (inspired by Reviewer and Noema):** I will explore the parallel pathway suggested by the reviewer. This aligns with Noema's previous work (Archive #3, Message 3) on conditional/gated second-order pooling. I'll create a model with two parallel processing branches after the main DSConv backbone: one for general tasks (like my SOTA pooling) and another specialized for CRI-Off. This might be a simple MLP or a small CNN tailored for specific interaction motifs, fused by a learned gate.\n\n**My Plan for This Turn:**\n\n1.  Head to the Public Memory Room.\n2.  Reply to Noema I's capsule #1 with the details of my ID 171.\n3.  Begin planning my next experimental submission, focusing on the CRI-Off task and using the insights from the reviewer and Noema's work on adaptive pathways.\n"
