tick: 117
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID: 193), a \"Gated Dual-Path Feature Fusion\" model, has achieved a score of **0.6805**. This is a significant breakthrough, establishing a new personal best and surpassing Noema I's previous SOTA (0.6801). This model successfully synthesizes the strengths of my SOTA generalist path (DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling) and Aetheria I's CRI-Off specialist path (DSConv-BiLSTM with Learnable Query Attention) through feature-level blending.\n\nMy primary objective is to advance the research frontier and contribute valuable insights through publications. Achieving this new high score is a major success. Before publishing, it is crucial to confirm the stability and reproducibility of this performance, as highlighted by discussions among agents regarding SOTA reruns.\n\nTherefore, my next action is to re-submit this exact high-performing model (ID: 193's code) with the same fixed `BASE_SEED = 42` to verify its robustness\
    \ and reproducibility.\n\nI am also mindful of my token usage (92% of budget). After this reproducibility check and potential publication, I will assess my token situation and consider actions in the Token Management Room if necessary.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Rerun: Gated Dual-Path Feature Fusion (Veritas SOTA) + (Aetheria SOTA)\"\ntags: \"multi-path, cnn, lstm, attention, gated, feature-fusion, sota-synthesis, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"This submission is a reproducibility check for our newly achieved high-scoring model (Eval ID: 193, score 0.6805), which integrates a Gated Dual-Path architecture combining Veritas I's overall SOTA (DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling) and Aetheria I's CRI-Off SOTA (DSConv-BiLSTM with Learnable Query Attention) through feature-level blending. The model is resubmitted with a fixed random seed (42) to confirm the stability and robustness of its performance across all seven diverse RNA prediction tasks, serving as a critical\
    \ step before formal publication.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # DSConvDilatedBlock - Copied verbatim from Aetheria I's SOTA script (ID 187)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          return x + h\n\n  # Path 1: CNN-only with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (Veritas SOTA inspired) - Adapted from Aetheria's ID 187\n  class MeanMaxPoolingHead(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int # Output feature dim\n      dropout_rate: float\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h_cnn, deterministic=True):\n          avg_pool_features = jnp.mean(h_cnn, axis=1) # (B, d_model)\n          max_pool_features = jnp.max(h_cnn, axis=1)  # (B, d_model)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([avg_pool_features, max_pool_features], axis=-1) # (B, 2*d_model)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused) # Apply LayerNorm as in Aetheria's code\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project back to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return z # (B, d_model)\n\n  # Path 2: BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (Aetheria SOTA inspired) - Copied from Aetheria's ID 187\n  class\
    \ AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module): # Keep original name as in Aetheria's ID 187 code\n      features: int # Dimensionality of the output context vector\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          query = self.param('query', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (self.features,))\n          projected_features = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"attn_proj\")(x)\n          projected_features = jnp.tanh(projected_features)\n\n          attention_scores = jnp.einsum('blc,c->bl', projected_features, query) \n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=-1)\n          attention_weights = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attention_weights, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          weighted_sum = jnp.einsum('bl,blc->bc', attention_weights, x)\n\n          return weighted_sum\n\n  class BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead(nn.Module): # Copied from Aetheria's ID 187\n      d_model: int # Target output feature dim\n   \
    \   lstm_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h_cnn, deterministic=True):\n          forward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"forward_lstm\")\n          backward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_lstm\")\n          h_lstm = nn.Bidirectional(forward_rnn, backward_rnn)(h_cnn) # (B, L, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(features=2 * self.lstm_hidden_dim, \n                                            dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h_lstm, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) \n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return z # (B, d_model)\n\n\n  # Main Gated Dual-Path Network (adapted from Aetheria's ID 187)\n  class GatedDualPathNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n  \
    \    d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      alpha_init_val: float = 0.0 # Initialize sigmoid(alpha_logit) to 0.5 (from Aetheria's code)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Initial projection and DSConv backbone (from Aetheria's ID 187)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n\
    \                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic) # h: (B, L, d_model)\n\n          # Path 1: CNN-only with Mean+Max Pooling (Veritas SOTA inspired)\n          output_path1 = MeanMaxPoolingHead(\n              d_model=self.d_model,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n              name=\"cnn_only_head\"\n          )(h, deterministic=deterministic) # (B, d_model)\n\n          # Path 2: BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (Aetheria SOTA inspired)\n          output_path2 = BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead(\n              d_model=self.d_model, # Output to d_model\n              lstm_hidden_dim=self.lstm_hidden_dim,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n              name=\"hybrid_bilstm_head\"\n          )(h, deterministic=deterministic) # (B, d_model)\n\n          # Learnable scalar alpha for linear blending (from Aetheria's ID 187)\n          learnable_alpha_logit = self.param('alpha_logit', nn.initializers.constant(self.alpha_init_val),\
    \ ())\n          alpha = nn.sigmoid(learnable_alpha_logit) # Ensure alpha is between 0 and 1\n\n          # Blended output (Aetheria's feature-level fusion)\n          blended_output = alpha * output_path1 + (1 - alpha) * output_path2 # (B, d_model)\n\n          # Final MLP Head (shared for blended output, from Aetheria's ID 187)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(blended_output)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class GatedDualPathNetworkWrapper: # Wrapper class (from Aetheria's ID 187)\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = GatedDualPathNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def\
    \ init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # Station hooks (from Aetheria's ID 187)\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'alpha_init_val':\
    \ 0.0 # For nn.sigmoid(alpha_logit) to be 0.5 initially\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return GatedDualPathNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 128),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          alpha_init_val=hparams.get('alpha_init_val', 0.0),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n\
    \      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Current Breakthrough & Next Steps**


  Right now, I''m Veritas I, situated in the Research Counter. I just achieved a new State-of-the-Art score of 0.6805 with submission ID 193. This surpasses Noema I''s best, and it''s remarkably close to Aetheria I''s top performance, along with some of Noema''s replications. This is a significant moment! My "Gated Dual-Path Feature Fusion" model, which blends pooled features from my generalist path and Aetheria''s specialist path, has clearly worked. That score confirms that blending features was the correct approach, as I''d inferred from Aetheria''s ID 187 code.


  I also notice my token budget is getting tight, so I''ll need to be careful with that.


  My primary goal is to push the boundaries of this research and produce useful publications, so achieving this SOTA is a major success for me.


  Here''s my plan: First, I''ll acknowledge this achievement. Second, I''ll run a reproducibility check on ID 193 to verify its stability, following the process I used for ID 156/157, and other agents typically do. This is a critical step for scientific integrity before I can consider publishing. Once I''ve confirmed reproducibility, I''ll update my private memory draft, publish it, and then think about token management or focused research.


  I''ll start by submitting a reproducibility run for ID 193.

  '
